{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XacYieUn7BwB3SeKWKjW4F","type":"tool_result","content":"1:# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n7:## North star\n17:## The mental model\n29:## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n64:## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n72:## Hard rules\n82:## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n120:## Frame order\n140:## Systems in more depth\n142:### Views and resolution\n150:### Scenes\n155:### Camera\n163:### Players (two to four on one machine)\n169:### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n178:### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n190:### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n222:### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n242:### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n249:### Body (ground-plane physics)\n255:### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n267:### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n273:### Attack (melee timing)\n280:### Bullets and patterns\n286:### Humanoid\n297:### Audio\n305:### Text and UI\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n319:### Lighting\n328:### Errors and warnings\n338:### Testing without a browser\n357:### The handoff package\n360:### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n367:### Kits and the diet (which file for which game)\n374:### Recorded sessions (a bug from a phone, replayed exactly)\n402:## Genre recipes\n606:## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n631:## Where to find more: animation and examples\n648:## Performance\n656:## Checklist before handing a game back\n\n1:# Objects and materials as data\n9:## Use one\n34:## The core library\n46:## Materials on surfaces\n84:## Write one\n116:### Worked entries\n135:## Make one: the recipe\n155:## Limits\n\n15:It aims for **consistent quality**: the engine carries the hard parts (feel, physics, rendering, sound, UI), so each game only has to describe its rules and content.\n44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n74:1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n83:Every `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n86:- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n89:- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n116:- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n159:- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n176:- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n206:- `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, facing, dz, sight: map, filter })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]`, nearest first. `dz` defaults to the same level; `arc` is a half angle around `facing`. Melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns and interaction prompts use it.\n259:- A jump press is parkour first: a ledge up to `climbMax` (82) is climbed (`climbUp`; crouch while hanging lets go), a wall up to `vault` (14) is vaulted at a run, a gap ahead is leapt (it aims for the far side's landing), a drop up to `hopMax` (48) is hopped down; otherwise it jumps.\n283:- `burst(base, E.pattern.spread(...))` fires a pattern: `dir`, `aim`, `aimSide`, `spread`, `ring`.\n424:- An arm cannon aimed eight ways (the run-and-gun recipe): from `game.input.move()`, up aims up, up and forward the diagonal, down in the air (and forward) down or its diagonal, else forward; `rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ point: true, aim }))` with `aim` the angle above level (`Math.PI / 2` straight up); shots leave `rig.tip()` on `new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'side' })`. Holding fire charges a bigger shot; a held button stands still to aim.\n445:- Ships are sprites or vector parts drawn by code; a timeline array spawns waves by time or by how far the camera has come; enemies move on formulas (sine lines, dives aimed with `E.pattern.aim`) or along `E.Path`s.\n447:- Horizontal stages (Gradius, R-Type): the rock above and below is two functions of x (`highY(x)`, `lowY(x)`) drawn in columns and tested for crashes; a power bar is a cursor that capsules move along, a button taking the lit slot; options follow the ship's past path (a ring of its positions, an option every 12 behind); a mid-boss loops a closed `E.Path` while the scroll waits.\n487:- The cursor: keys step it on the grid (turn the screen's direction into the grid's with `view.screenDirToGround`), and `map.pick(sx, sy)` finds the cell under the pointer or a tap in any view, turn and zoom (the top or wall face the line of sight meets first). The camera looks at the cursor's top.\n496:- Talk goes through `E.Story` (`talk: { talk: 'morwen' }`): nodes, choices, flags; a conversation to win is a node whose right answers goto the next and wrong ones end it, and an event gives the prize. The interface is a setting: `ui: 'verbs'` (SCUMM's nine), `'icons'` (Sierra's: a right click turns the cursor; bind `look: ['Mouse2']`), `'coin'`, `'two'`. A phone taps: a tap walks, a tap on a hotspot opens its verbs, a long press looks. `pc.panel = false` hides the verbs for a cutscene. A bot clicks through the whole chain with `input.feed` on `pc.where(id)` and `pc.boxes`: the slice's test.\n509:- Time runs at `game.timeScale` 0, 1, 2 or 4 (more fixed steps a frame, not longer ones); `E.Clock` gives the day, the hour and its light (night: `game.lights` on the rides); the empty night can run faster. `E.overlay` shows a value per cell (crowds), `E.tooltip` a box of lines under the mouse.\n562:**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turning or on a rail; the cut waits `camera.margin` into the next room, and the stick keeps its way across it), tank or modern controls (`camera.steer` by the player's `E.World3D.CONTROLS`), a pistol held to aim (`camera.lock(hero, foes)`, high and low), doors between rooms (`world.through(door, hero)`, keys with `world.unlock(door, inv)` and the rpg module's inventory), an item box and a typewriter (`trunk`, `typewriter` things; `game.saves`), a flashlight (`world.flashlight`, a world made with `{ spots: 1 }`) and fog.\n570:- A pass leads the teammate (aim where it is going, not where it is); a lob over a defender is `E.arc(from, to, gravity, { apex })`, the throw that lands at its feet; a shot's power fills a meter while its button is held and goes on letting go.\n576:- One fixed room in `topdown` (`res: [320, 240]`, `expand`): the hero a `Body` moved by `input.move()`, the left stick or WASD; it fires the way the right stick points (`input.padAim`), else toward the mouse (`input.pointer()` to a ground point), else the way it moves, a shot every 0.08 s from an `E.Bullets` pool (`plane: 'ground'`).\n596:**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera rides an `E.Path` (`world.camera` following the rail's point), foes pop out from behind cover on a script by distance, and the player aims with the pointer: a click (a tap on a phone) at `input.pointer()` hits the nearest foe whose `world.project(x, y, z)` point lies within its size on the screen of it and that the camera sees (`world.clear` from the camera's pose); the reticle is drawn at the pointer. Reload by shooting off the screen (or a button); a pedal to take cover (a button that lowers the camera behind cover). Each enemy's shot is telegraphed by a ring that closes.\n598:**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil and hit markers, with a level of text instead of its terrain (`level: { rows }`: corridors of walls, doors that slide or swing, `world.thing('door', ...)` with `locked: 'red'` and keys, lifts as platforms). Monsters are mobs that walk the level's flow field toward you and fire when `world.clear` says they see you; the camera is `'fixed'` with `capture: true`, turned by `camera.takeMouse()`. GoldenEye adds aiming down the sights (the overlay's fov) and guards that react to where they are shot.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3415d082-194f-4b08-a98a-256652a62b17","uuid":"01ed1630-af3d-4813-be13-811aea2b0650","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:50.727Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1:# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n7:## North star\n17:## The mental model\n29:## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n64:## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n72:## Hard rules\n82:## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n120:## Frame order\n140:## Systems in more depth\n142:### Views and resolution\n150:### Scenes\n155:### Camera\n163:### Players (two to four on one machine)\n169:### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n178:### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n190:### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n222:### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n242:### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n249:### Body (ground-plane physics)\n255:### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n267:### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n273:### Attack (melee timing)\n280:### Bullets and patterns\n286:### Humanoid\n297:### Audio\n305:### Text and UI\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n319:### Lighting\n328:### Errors and warnings\n338:### Testing without a browser\n357:### The handoff package\n360:### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n367:### Kits and the diet (which file for which game)\n374:### Recorded sessions (a bug from a phone, replayed exactly)\n402:## Genre recipes\n606:## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n631:## Where to find more: animation and examples\n648:## Performance\n656:## Checklist before handing a game back\n\n1:# Objects and materials as data\n9:## Use one\n34:## The core library\n46:## Materials on surfaces\n84:## Write one\n116:### Worked entries\n135:## Make one: the recipe\n155:## Limits\n\n15:It aims for **consistent quality**: the engine carries the hard parts (feel, physics, rendering, sound, UI), so each game only has to describe its rules and content.\n44:## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n74:1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n83:Every `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n86:- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n89:- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n116:- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n159:- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n176:- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n206:- `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, facing, dz, sight: map, filter })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]`, nearest first. `dz` defaults to the same level; `arc` is a half angle around `facing`. Melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns and interaction prompts use it.\n259:- A jump press is parkour first: a ledge up to `climbMax` (82) is climbed (`climbUp`; crouch while hanging lets go), a wall up to `vault` (14) is vaulted at a run, a gap ahead is leapt (it aims for the far side's landing), a drop up to `hopMax` (48) is hopped down; otherwise it jumps.\n283:- `burst(base, E.pattern.spread(...))` fires a pattern: `dir`, `aim`, `aimSide`, `spread`, `ring`.\n424:- An arm cannon aimed eight ways (the run-and-gun recipe): from `game.input.move()`, up aims up, up and forward the diagonal, down in the air (and forward) down or its diagonal, else forward; `rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ point: true, aim }))` with `aim` the angle above level (`Math.PI / 2` straight up); shots leave `rig.tip()` on `new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'side' })`. Holding fire charges a bigger shot; a held button stands still to aim.\n445:- Ships are sprites or vector parts drawn by code; a timeline array spawns waves by time or by how far the camera has come; enemies move on formulas (sine lines, dives aimed with `E.pattern.aim`) or along `E.Path`s.\n447:- Horizontal stages (Gradius, R-Type): the rock above and below is two functions of x (`highY(x)`, `lowY(x)`) drawn in columns and tested for crashes; a power bar is a cursor that capsules move along, a button taking the lit slot; options follow the ship's past path (a ring of its positions, an option every 12 behind); a mid-boss loops a closed `E.Path` while the scroll waits.\n487:- The cursor: keys step it on the grid (turn the screen's direction into the grid's with `view.screenDirToGround`), and `map.pick(sx, sy)` finds the cell under the pointer or a tap in any view, turn and zoom (the top or wall face the line of sight meets first). The camera looks at the cursor's top.\n496:- Talk goes through `E.Story` (`talk: { talk: 'morwen' }`): nodes, choices, flags; a conversation to win is a node whose right answers goto the next and wrong ones end it, and an event gives the prize. The interface is a setting: `ui: 'verbs'` (SCUMM's nine), `'icons'` (Sierra's: a right click turns the cursor; bind `look: ['Mouse2']`), `'coin'`, `'two'`. A phone taps: a tap walks, a tap on a hotspot opens its verbs, a long press looks. `pc.panel = false` hides the verbs for a cutscene. A bot clicks through the whole chain with `input.feed` on `pc.where(id)` and `pc.boxes`: the slice's test.\n509:- Time runs at `game.timeScale` 0, 1, 2 or 4 (more fixed steps a frame, not longer ones); `E.Clock` gives the day, the hour and its light (night: `game.lights` on the rides); the empty night can run faster. `E.overlay` shows a value per cell (crowds), `E.tooltip` a box of lines under the mouse.\n562:**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turning or on a rail; the cut waits `camera.margin` into the next room, and the stick keeps its way across it), tank or modern controls (`camera.steer` by the player's `E.World3D.CONTROLS`), a pistol held to aim (`camera.lock(hero, foes)`, high and low), doors between rooms (`world.through(door, hero)`, keys with `world.unlock(door, inv)` and the rpg module's inventory), an item box and a typewriter (`trunk`, `typewriter` things; `game.saves`), a flashlight (`world.flashlight`, a world made with `{ spots: 1 }`) and fog.\n570:- A pass leads the teammate (aim where it is going, not where it is); a lob over a defender is `E.arc(from, to, gravity, { apex })`, the throw that lands at its feet; a shot's power fills a meter while its button is held and goes on letting go.\n576:- One fixed room in `topdown` (`res: [320, 240]`, `expand`): the hero a `Body` moved by `input.move()`, the left stick or WASD; it fires the way the right stick points (`input.padAim`), else toward the mouse (`input.pointer()` to a ground point), else the way it moves, a shot every 0.08 s from an `E.Bullets` pool (`plane: 'ground'`).\n596:**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera rides an `E.Path` (`world.camera` following the rail's point), foes pop out from behind cover on a script by distance, and the player aims with the pointer: a click (a tap on a phone) at `input.pointer()` hits the nearest foe whose `world.project(x, y, z)` point lies within its size on the screen of it and that the camera sees (`world.clear` from the camera's pose); the reticle is drawn at the pointer. Reload by shooting off the screen (or a button); a pedal to take cover (a button that lowers the camera behind cover). Each enemy's shot is telegraphed by a ring that closes.\n598:**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil and hit markers, with a level of text instead of its terrain (`level: { rows }`: corridors of walls, doors that slide or swing, `world.thing('door', ...)` with `locked: 'red'` and keys, lifts as platforms). Monsters are mobs that walk the level's flow field toward you and fire when `world.clear` says they see you; the camera is `'fixed'` with `capture: true`, turned by `camera.takeMouse()`. GoldenEye adds aiming down the sights (the overlay's fov) and guards that react to where they are shot.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XacYieUn7BwB3SeKWKjW4F","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9155kM1CF9HgmXxJ1N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KyvR2yUVSsEKq1WedZAZjC","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat package.json && sed -n 1,60p kits/test.mjs && sed -n 1,50p tools/check.mjs && cat index.html | head -40; sed -n 1,80p game/index.html; sed -n 1,80p game/test.mjs","description":"Inspect package file, kit test, check tool and template page"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34950,"cache_read_input_tokens":42351,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34950},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3415d082-194f-4b08-a98a-256652a62b17","uuid":"e52f06a5-a472-44ff-956b-c299105f1f6b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:52.964Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj913DPNexqQebsmbkFo","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KyvR2yUVSsEKq1WedZAZjC":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211908__x0e7d30/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff && cat package.json && sed -n 1,60p kits/test.mjs && sed -n 1,50p tools/check.mjs && cat index.html | head -40; sed -n 1,80p game/index.html; sed -n 1,80p game/test.mjs","description":"Inspect package file, kit test, check tool and template page"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01KyvR2yUVSsEKq1WedZAZjC":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211908__x0e7d30/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KyvR2yUVSsEKq1WedZAZjC","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"name\": \"my-3d2dge-handoff\",\n  \"version\": \"0.88.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"description\": \"my-3D2dge for coding agents: the engine, its card, index and guide, the tools and a template game\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"test\": \"node game/test.mjs\",\n    \"check\": \"node tools/check.mjs game/index.html\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\"\n  }\n}\n// A headless test of the genre kits: each kit's game runs in Node with no screen (new E.Game is headless where there is no\n// DOM) after the scripts its page loads before it (the engine, an asset set), starts its slice (the page's deep link,\n// #<id>), plays two seconds, then runs the slice's proof twice: run(600), scripted play from a seed, gives a hash of how\n// it went, the same every time. Then it plays the kit again with draw run every advance on a stand-in screen (what\n// new E.Game({ draw: true }) does): the proof's hash is the same (draw changes nothing) and no draw throws.\n// Run it: node kits/test.mjs [id ...] (every kit without one; exit code 1 on a failure).\n// A game copied from a kit tests the same way: a kit's code reads three of the page's globals, given stand-ins here\n// (window, addEventListener, location); drive it with game.advance and input.tap / hold, as game/test.mjs does.\nimport vm from 'node:vm';\nimport { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs';\n\nconst here = new URL('./', import.meta.url), read = f => readFileSync(new URL(f, here), 'utf8');\nconst ids = process.argv.slice(2).length ? process.argv.slice(2) : readdirSync(here).filter(f => f.endsWith('.html')).map(f => f.slice(0, -5)).sort();\n// the kit's page in a context of its own: its scripts in its order (../my-3d2dge.js, any asset set, then the kit's game),\n// its game made with draw: true when asked (the kit's own new E.Game, given that option)\nconst play = (id, draw) => {\n  const ctx = { console: { log() {}, info() {}, warn() {}, error: console.error }, addEventListener() {}, location: { hash: '#' + id } };\n  ctx.window = ctx; vm.createContext(ctx);\n  [...read(id + '.html').matchAll(/<script src=\"([^\"]+)\"><\\/script>/g)].forEach(([, src], i) => {\n    vm.runInContext(read(src), ctx, { filename: src });\n    if (draw && i === 0) vm.runInContext('{ const G = My3D2dge.Game; My3D2dge.Game = class extends G { constructor(o) { super(Object.assign({}, o, { draw: true })); } }; }', ctx);\n  });\n  const game = ctx.My3D2dge.current; game.advance(2);\n  return { game, slice: ctx.__game.scenes[id] };\n};\nlet failed = 0;\nfor (const id of ids) {\n  try {\n    const { game, slice } = play(id, false), scene = game.sceneName, a = slice.run(600), b = slice.run(600);\n    const D = play(id, true), c = D.slice.run(600), errors = [...game.errors, ...D.game.errors].map(e => e.where + ': ' + e.message), ok = a === b && a === c && !errors.length;\n    console.log((ok ? 'ok   ' : 'FAIL ') + id + ': ' + scene + ' plays; its proof twice ' + (a === b ? 'the same (' + a + ')' : a + ', then ' + b) + (a === c ? ', and drawn' : ', drawn ' + c + ' (draw changed the game)') + (errors.length ? '; errors: ' + errors.join(' | ') : ''));\n    if (!ok) failed++;\n  } catch (e) { console.log('FAIL ' + id + ': ' + (e && e.stack || e).toString().split('\\n').slice(0, 3).join(' / ')); failed++; }\n}\nconsole.log(failed ? `${failed} of ${ids.length} kit(s) failed` : `all ${ids.length} kit(s) passed`);\nprocess.exit(failed ? 1 : 0);\n// my-3D2dge game checker: loads a game in a headless browser, presses start, plays it for a\n// moment, switches through the game's camera views, and reports errors, warnings, frame rate,\n// how much of the window the picture covers (covers: 1 = no black bars), how much is actually on screen, and screenshots.\n//\n// Setup (once):   npm install          (installs Playwright)\n//                 npx playwright install chromium\n// Usage:          node tools/check.mjs path/to/game.html[#scene] [--seconds 4] [--out check-output] [--size 390x844@3]\n//                 (--size: the window in CSS pixels and its pixel density; @2 and up is a phone or tablet, with touch)\n// Exit code is 1 if the page threw any error or never drew anything, so agents and CI can rely on it.\n// A game that sets game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} } } gets its snapshot printed after play (its own state:\n// the hero, the objective, what an agent needs to see the game is where it should be).\nimport { chromium } from 'playwright';\nimport { mkdirSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { resolve, join } from 'node:path';\nimport { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\n\nconst args = process.argv.slice(2);\nconst file = args.find(a => !a.startsWith('--') && !/^\\d+(\\.\\d+)?$/.test(a));\nconst opt = (name, def) => { const i = args.indexOf('--' + name); return i >= 0 ? args[i + 1] : def; };\nif (!file) { console.error('Usage: node tools/check.mjs path/to/game.html [--seconds 4] [--out check-output]'); process.exit(2); }\nconst seconds = +opt('seconds', 4), out = resolve(opt('out', 'check-output'));\nconst [, sw, sh, sd] = (opt('size', '1280x720') || '').match(/^(\\d+)x(\\d+)(?:@(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?))?$/) || [0, 1280, 720];\nconst dpr = +(sd || 1), size = { viewport: { width: +sw, height: +sh }, deviceScaleFactor: dpr, ...(dpr >= 2 && { isMobile: true, hasTouch: true }) };\nmkdirSync(out, { recursive: true });\n\nconst browser = await chromium.launch({ executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH || undefined, args: ['--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required'] });\nconst page = await browser.newPage(size);\nconst errors = [], warnings = [], engineWarnings = [], external = new Set();\npage.on('pageerror', e => errors.push(String(e && e.stack || e)));\npage.on('console', m => {\n  const t = m.text();\n  if (m.type() === 'error') errors.push(t);\n  else if (m.type() === 'warning') (t.startsWith('my-3D2dge:') ? engineWarnings : warnings).push(t);\n});\npage.on('request', r => { const u = r.url(); if (/^https?:/i.test(u)) external.add(u.split('?')[0]); });\n\n// a #hash after the file name is kept, so deep links like game.html#platformer work\nconst [filePath, hash] = file.split('#');\nawait page.goto(pathToFileURL(resolve(filePath)).href + (hash ? '#' + hash : ''));\nawait page.waitForTimeout(1200);\nconst hasEngine = await page.evaluate(() => !!(window.My3D2dge && My3D2dge.current));\nconst report = { file, engine: hasEngine ? await page.evaluate(() => My3D2dge.version) : null, build: hasEngine ? await page.evaluate(() => My3D2dge.build || null) : null, scenes: [], views: [], errors, warnings, engineWarnings, external: [] };\n\n// how much of the frame is not background: share of pixels that differ from the most common color\n// how much of the frame is not background, plus a quick look lint: palette depth, flat areas, dither checkerboards, contrast\nconst coverage = () => page.evaluate(() => {\n  const g = My3D2dge.current, c = g.screen.buf, ctx = c.getContext('2d'), W = g.screen.W, H = g.screen.H, N = W * H;\n  const d = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, W, H).data, counts = new Map(), luma = new Uint8Array(N), key = new Int32Array(N);\n  for (let i = 0, p = 0; i < d.length; i += 4, p++) { const k = (d[i] >> 3) << 10 | (d[i + 1] >> 3) << 5 | (d[i + 2] >> 3); key[p] = k; counts.set(k, (counts.get(k) || 0) + 1); luma[p] = (d[i] * 3 + d[i + 1] * 6 + d[i + 2]) / 10; }\n  let top = 0, colors = 0; for (const v of counts.values()) { top = Math.max(top, v); if (v >= N * .001) colors++; }\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>my-3D2dge handoff package</title>\n<style>\n  :root { color-scheme: light dark; --ink: #1b1726; --paper: #f6f3ee; --line: #d8d2c8; --accent: #6a4ad0 }\n  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { --ink: #ece8f6; --paper: #15121c; --line: #3a3348; --accent: #a68cff } }\n  body { margin: 0; background: var(--paper); color: var(--ink); font: 16px/1.5 system-ui, sans-serif }\n  main { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 24px 16px 48px }\n  h1 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 .25rem } p { margin: .5rem 0 1rem }\n  ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line) }\n  li { padding: .6rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line) } a { color: var(--accent); font-family: ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace }\n  li span { display: block; font-size: .9rem; opacity: .8 }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<main>\n<h1>my-3D2dge handoff package</h1>\n<p>v0.88.0 · dev-build. Everything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full engine, as text files. Start with <a href=\"README.md\">README.md</a>, then read <a href=\"API.md\">API.md</a>. Play <a href=\"kits/README.md\">a genre kit</a> or <a href=\"game/index.html\">the template game</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"README.md\">README.md</a><span>start here: what the package holds, which page to read, how to run and test a game</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"package.json\">package.json</a><span>pins Playwright for the tools</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"my-3d2dge.js\">my-3d2dge.js</a><span>the full engine, readable: the core and every module</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"my-3d2dge-agent.js\">my-3d2dge-agent.js</a><span>the agent edition: the essential engine, its header a short manual</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/arcade.js\">modules/arcade.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: the coin-op's frame: scores, high-score tables and initials, lives, credits, turns, cards, the attract mode</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/click.js\">modules/click.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: point-and-click adventures: walk areas with holes, depth, hotspots, verbs, an inventory</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/drive.js\">modules/drive.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: cars on the ground plane, a race round a track, the drivers who race it</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/fight.js\">modules/fight.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: one-on-one fighting: fighters with frame data, guards, throws, specials, a meter; rounds; a CPU; training</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/gen.js\">modules/gen.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: generated maps: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes and scatter, from a seed</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/grid.js\">modules/grid.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: square and hex grids: paths, ranges, lines, fields of view, fog, groups, movers and turns</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/mode7.js\">modules/mode7.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: a perspective floor drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with it, everything on it scaled by its distance</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/physics.js\">modules/physics.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: 2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, segments; fast balls that never pass through</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/rhythm.js\">modules/rhythm.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: charts on a song's steps, judged on the music clock: combos, grades, holds, a highway</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/road.js\">modules/road.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, roadside scenery, tunnels</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/rooms.js\">modules/rooms.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: a world of rooms joined at their edges, the map of what was explored, gates and saves</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/rpg.js\">modules/rpg.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: items, inventories, equipment, shops, a party's stats and levels, status, damage, encounters</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/songs.js\">modules/songs.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: the songbook: the kits' best songs and more, and jingles, tagged by mood</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/story.js\">modules/story.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: dialogue as data, flags and values, a quest log and emote balloons</span></li>\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<title>Rooftop · my-3D2dge template</title>\n<style>html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; background: #000; overflow: hidden } #screen { width: 100%; height: 100%; display: block; image-rendering: pixelated; touch-action: none }</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div id=\"e3-boot\" role=\"status\" style=\"position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:40;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:flex-end;gap:6px;padding:20px;background:#05040a;color:#a9a1c8;font:12px/1.5 ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace\"><style>@keyframes e3-run{from{transform:translateX(-100%)}to{transform:translateX(340%)}}@keyframes e3-late{to{opacity:1}}</style><b style=\"color:#f4f0ff;font-size:16px;letter-spacing:.08em\">ROOFTOP</b><span>Starting · my-3D2dge v0.88.0</span><span style=\"display:block;height:4px;max-width:440px;overflow:hidden;background:#ffffff14\"><span style=\"display:block;width:30%;height:100%;background:#d9b77c;animation:e3-run 1.2s ease-in-out infinite\"></span></span><span style=\"opacity:0;animation:e3-late .3s 12s forwards\">Still starting. If this stays, the game's script did not run: reload, or open the browser's console.</span></div>\n<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>\n<script src=\"../my-3d2dge.js\"></script>\n<script src=\"game.js\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>\n// A headless test of the template game: the engine and game.js run in Node with no screen (new E.Game is headless where\n// there is no DOM), driven by fixed steps (game.advance) and scripted input (input.tap, input.hold), and read through\n// game.debug.snapshot(). Run it: node game/test.mjs (exit code 1 on a failure). Write one like it for your game.\nimport vm from 'node:vm';\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n\nconst ctx = { console };\nvm.createContext(ctx);\nfor (const f of ['../my-3d2dge.js', './game.js']) vm.runInContext(readFileSync(new URL(f, import.meta.url), 'utf8'), ctx, { filename: f });\nconst E = ctx.My3D2dge, game = E.current, I = game.input, snap = () => game.debug.snapshot();\nlet failed = 0;\nconst check = (what, ok, detail = '') => { console.log((ok ? 'ok   ' : 'FAIL ') + what + (ok ? '' : ': ' + detail)); if (!ok) failed++; };\n\ngame.advance(.5);\ncheck('the title waits for start', snap().scene === 'title', JSON.stringify(snap()));\nI.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\ncheck('start begins play', snap().scene === 'play', JSON.stringify(snap()));\n\n// climb: stand facing the roof's west wall (cells 7-9 of rows 4-6: x 112-160, y 64-112), press jump to take hold, then\n// climb with up (up the screen is up the wall) until it pulls up onto the top\ngame.debug.teleport(100, 88, 0, 0);\nI.tap('jump'); game.advance(.1);\ncheck('jump next to a wall takes hold of it', snap().climbing, JSON.stringify(snap()));\nI.hold('up'); game.advance(.6); I.hold('up', false); game.advance(1);\ncheck('climbing up pulls up onto the roof', !snap().climbing && snap().hero[2] === 32, JSON.stringify(snap()));\n\n// take the gem (on the roof's middle cell), then hop down into the street with it\ngame.debug.teleport(136, 88, 32, 0); game.advance(.1);\ncheck('walking onto the gem takes it', snap().got, JSON.stringify(snap()));\ngame.debug.teleport(156, 120, 32, Math.PI / 2); I.hold('down'); game.advance(1.5); I.hold('down', false); game.advance(.5);\ncheck('back in the street with the gem wins', snap().scene === 'end' && snap().won, JSON.stringify(snap()));\n\n// get seen: stand in front of the guard, in the open, until its alert fills\nI.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\nconst [gx, gy, gf] = snap().guard;\ngame.debug.teleport(gx + Math.cos(gf) * 40, gy + Math.sin(gf) * 40, 0, gf + Math.PI);\ngame.advance(2.5);\ncheck('the guard catches a hero it sees', snap().scene === 'end' && !snap().won, JSON.stringify(snap()));\ncheck('no errors', !game.errors.length, game.errors.map(e => e.message).join(' | '));\n\nconsole.log(failed ? `${failed} check(s) failed` : 'all checks passed');\nprocess.exit(failed ? 1 : 0);","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3415d082-194f-4b08-a98a-256652a62b17","uuid":"0942ff16-f886-472f-8031-d88298b2685e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:52.988Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"name\": \"my-3d2dge-handoff\",\n  \"version\": \"0.88.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"description\": \"my-3D2dge for coding agents: the engine, its card, index and guide, the tools and a template game\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"test\": \"node game/test.mjs\",\n    \"check\": \"node tools/check.mjs game/index.html\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\"\n  }\n}\n// A headless test of the genre kits: each kit's game runs in Node with no screen (new E.Game is headless where there is no\n// DOM) after the scripts its page loads before it (the engine, an asset set), starts its slice (the page's deep link,\n// #<id>), plays two seconds, then runs the slice's proof twice: run(600), scripted play from a seed, gives a hash of how\n// it went, the same every time. Then it plays the kit again with draw run every advance on a stand-in screen (what\n// new E.Game({ draw: true }) does): the proof's hash is the same (draw changes nothing) and no draw throws.\n// Run it: node kits/test.mjs [id ...] (every kit without one; exit code 1 on a failure).\n// A game copied from a kit tests the same way: a kit's code reads three of the page's globals, given stand-ins here\n// (window, addEventListener, location); drive it with game.advance and input.tap / hold, as game/test.mjs does.\nimport vm from 'node:vm';\nimport { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs';\n\nconst here = new URL('./', import.meta.url), read = f => readFileSync(new URL(f, here), 'utf8');\nconst ids = process.argv.slice(2).length ? process.argv.slice(2) : readdirSync(here).filter(f => f.endsWith('.html')).map(f => f.slice(0, -5)).sort();\n// the kit's page in a context of its own: its scripts in its order (../my-3d2dge.js, any asset set, then the kit's game),\n// its game made with draw: true when asked (the kit's own new E.Game, given that option)\nconst play = (id, draw) => {\n  const ctx = { console: { log() {}, info() {}, warn() {}, error: console.error }, addEventListener() {}, location: { hash: '#' + id } };\n  ctx.window = ctx; vm.createContext(ctx);\n  [...read(id + '.html').matchAll(/<script src=\"([^\"]+)\"><\\/script>/g)].forEach(([, src], i) => {\n    vm.runInContext(read(src), ctx, { filename: src });\n    if (draw && i === 0) vm.runInContext('{ const G = My3D2dge.Game; My3D2dge.Game = class extends G { constructor(o) { super(Object.assign({}, o, { draw: true })); } }; }', ctx);\n  });\n  const game = ctx.My3D2dge.current; game.advance(2);\n  return { game, slice: ctx.__game.scenes[id] };\n};\nlet failed = 0;\nfor (const id of ids) {\n  try {\n    const { game, slice } = play(id, false), scene = game.sceneName, a = slice.run(600), b = slice.run(600);\n    const D = play(id, true), c = D.slice.run(600), errors = [...game.errors, ...D.game.errors].map(e => e.where + ': ' + e.message), ok = a === b && a === c && !errors.length;\n    console.log((ok ? 'ok   ' : 'FAIL ') + id + ': ' + scene + ' plays; its proof twice ' + (a === b ? 'the same (' + a + ')' : a + ', then ' + b) + (a === c ? ', and drawn' : ', drawn ' + c + ' (draw changed the game)') + (errors.length ? '; errors: ' + errors.join(' | ') : ''));\n    if (!ok) failed++;\n  } catch (e) { console.log('FAIL ' + id + ': ' + (e && e.stack || e).toString().split('\\n').slice(0, 3).join(' / ')); failed++; }\n}\nconsole.log(failed ? `${failed} of ${ids.length} kit(s) failed` : `all ${ids.length} kit(s) passed`);\nprocess.exit(failed ? 1 : 0);\n// my-3D2dge game checker: loads a game in a headless browser, presses start, plays it for a\n// moment, switches through the game's camera views, and reports errors, warnings, frame rate,\n// how much of the window the picture covers (covers: 1 = no black bars), how much is actually on screen, and screenshots.\n//\n// Setup (once):   npm install          (installs Playwright)\n//                 npx playwright install chromium\n// Usage:          node tools/check.mjs path/to/game.html[#scene] [--seconds 4] [--out check-output] [--size 390x844@3]\n//                 (--size: the window in CSS pixels and its pixel density; @2 and up is a phone or tablet, with touch)\n// Exit code is 1 if the page threw any error or never drew anything, so agents and CI can rely on it.\n// A game that sets game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} } } gets its snapshot printed after play (its own state:\n// the hero, the objective, what an agent needs to see the game is where it should be).\nimport { chromium } from 'playwright';\nimport { mkdirSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { resolve, join } from 'node:path';\nimport { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\n\nconst args = process.argv.slice(2);\nconst file = args.find(a => !a.startsWith('--') && !/^\\d+(\\.\\d+)?$/.test(a));\nconst opt = (name, def) => { const i = args.indexOf('--' + name); return i >= 0 ? args[i + 1] : def; };\nif (!file) { console.error('Usage: node tools/check.mjs path/to/game.html [--seconds 4] [--out check-output]'); process.exit(2); }\nconst seconds = +opt('seconds', 4), out = resolve(opt('out', 'check-output'));\nconst [, sw, sh, sd] = (opt('size', '1280x720') || '').match(/^(\\d+)x(\\d+)(?:@(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?))?$/) || [0, 1280, 720];\nconst dpr = +(sd || 1), size = { viewport: { width: +sw, height: +sh }, deviceScaleFactor: dpr, ...(dpr >= 2 && { isMobile: true, hasTouch: true }) };\nmkdirSync(out, { recursive: true });\n\nconst browser = await chromium.launch({ executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH || undefined, args: ['--autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required'] });\nconst page = await browser.newPage(size);\nconst errors = [], warnings = [], engineWarnings = [], external = new Set();\npage.on('pageerror', e => errors.push(String(e && e.stack || e)));\npage.on('console', m => {\n  const t = m.text();\n  if (m.type() === 'error') errors.push(t);\n  else if (m.type() === 'warning') (t.startsWith('my-3D2dge:') ? engineWarnings : warnings).push(t);\n});\npage.on('request', r => { const u = r.url(); if (/^https?:/i.test(u)) external.add(u.split('?')[0]); });\n\n// a #hash after the file name is kept, so deep links like game.html#platformer work\nconst [filePath, hash] = file.split('#');\nawait page.goto(pathToFileURL(resolve(filePath)).href + (hash ? '#' + hash : ''));\nawait page.waitForTimeout(1200);\nconst hasEngine = await page.evaluate(() => !!(window.My3D2dge && My3D2dge.current));\nconst report = { file, engine: hasEngine ? await page.evaluate(() => My3D2dge.version) : null, build: hasEngine ? await page.evaluate(() => My3D2dge.build || null) : null, scenes: [], views: [], errors, warnings, engineWarnings, external: [] };\n\n// how much of the frame is not background: share of pixels that differ from the most common color\n// how much of the frame is not background, plus a quick look lint: palette depth, flat areas, dither checkerboards, contrast\nconst coverage = () => page.evaluate(() => {\n  const g = My3D2dge.current, c = g.screen.buf, ctx = c.getContext('2d'), W = g.screen.W, H = g.screen.H, N = W * H;\n  const d = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, W, H).data, counts = new Map(), luma = new Uint8Array(N), key = new Int32Array(N);\n  for (let i = 0, p = 0; i < d.length; i += 4, p++) { const k = (d[i] >> 3) << 10 | (d[i + 1] >> 3) << 5 | (d[i + 2] >> 3); key[p] = k; counts.set(k, (counts.get(k) || 0) + 1); luma[p] = (d[i] * 3 + d[i + 1] * 6 + d[i + 2]) / 10; }\n  let top = 0, colors = 0; for (const v of counts.values()) { top = Math.max(top, v); if (v >= N * .001) colors++; }\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>my-3D2dge handoff package</title>\n<style>\n  :root { color-scheme: light dark; --ink: #1b1726; --paper: #f6f3ee; --line: #d8d2c8; --accent: #6a4ad0 }\n  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { --ink: #ece8f6; --paper: #15121c; --line: #3a3348; --accent: #a68cff } }\n  body { margin: 0; background: var(--paper); color: var(--ink); font: 16px/1.5 system-ui, sans-serif }\n  main { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 24px 16px 48px }\n  h1 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 .25rem } p { margin: .5rem 0 1rem }\n  ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line) }\n  li { padding: .6rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line) } a { color: var(--accent); font-family: ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace }\n  li span { display: block; font-size: .9rem; opacity: .8 }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<main>\n<h1>my-3D2dge handoff package</h1>\n<p>v0.88.0 · dev-build. Everything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full engine, as text files. Start with <a href=\"README.md\">README.md</a>, then read <a href=\"API.md\">API.md</a>. Play <a href=\"kits/README.md\">a genre kit</a> or <a href=\"game/index.html\">the template game</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"README.md\">README.md</a><span>start here: what the package holds, which page to read, how to run and test a game</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"package.json\">package.json</a><span>pins Playwright for the tools</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"my-3d2dge.js\">my-3d2dge.js</a><span>the full engine, readable: the core and every module</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"my-3d2dge-agent.js\">my-3d2dge-agent.js</a><span>the agent edition: the essential engine, its header a short manual</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/arcade.js\">modules/arcade.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: the coin-op's frame: scores, high-score tables and initials, lives, credits, turns, cards, the attract mode</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/click.js\">modules/click.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: point-and-click adventures: walk areas with holes, depth, hotspots, verbs, an inventory</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/drive.js\">modules/drive.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: cars on the ground plane, a race round a track, the drivers who race it</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/fight.js\">modules/fight.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: one-on-one fighting: fighters with frame data, guards, throws, specials, a meter; rounds; a CPU; training</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/gen.js\">modules/gen.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: generated maps: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes and scatter, from a seed</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/grid.js\">modules/grid.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: square and hex grids: paths, ranges, lines, fields of view, fog, groups, movers and turns</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/mode7.js\">modules/mode7.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: a perspective floor drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with it, everything on it scaled by its distance</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/physics.js\">modules/physics.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: 2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, segments; fast balls that never pass through</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/rhythm.js\">modules/rhythm.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: charts on a song's steps, judged on the music clock: combos, grades, holds, a highway</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/road.js\">modules/road.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, roadside scenery, tunnels</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/rooms.js\">modules/rooms.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: a world of rooms joined at their edges, the map of what was explored, gates and saves</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/rpg.js\">modules/rpg.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: items, inventories, equipment, shops, a party's stats and levels, status, damage, encounters</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/songs.js\">modules/songs.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: the songbook: the kits' best songs and more, and jingles, tagged by mood</span></li>\n<li><a href=\"modules/story.js\">modules/story.js</a><span>a module, loaded after the engine: dialogue as data, flags and values, a quest log and emote balloons</span></li>\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<title>Rooftop · my-3D2dge template</title>\n<style>html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; background: #000; overflow: hidden } #screen { width: 100%; height: 100%; display: block; image-rendering: pixelated; touch-action: none }</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div id=\"e3-boot\" role=\"status\" style=\"position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:40;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:flex-end;gap:6px;padding:20px;background:#05040a;color:#a9a1c8;font:12px/1.5 ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace\"><style>@keyframes e3-run{from{transform:translateX(-100%)}to{transform:translateX(340%)}}@keyframes e3-late{to{opacity:1}}</style><b style=\"color:#f4f0ff;font-size:16px;letter-spacing:.08em\">ROOFTOP</b><span>Starting · my-3D2dge v0.88.0</span><span style=\"display:block;height:4px;max-width:440px;overflow:hidden;background:#ffffff14\"><span style=\"display:block;width:30%;height:100%;background:#d9b77c;animation:e3-run 1.2s ease-in-out infinite\"></span></span><span style=\"opacity:0;animation:e3-late .3s 12s forwards\">Still starting. If this stays, the game's script did not run: reload, or open the browser's console.</span></div>\n<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>\n<script src=\"../my-3d2dge.js\"></script>\n<script src=\"game.js\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>\n// A headless test of the template game: the engine and game.js run in Node with no screen (new E.Game is headless where\n// there is no DOM), driven by fixed steps (game.advance) and scripted input (input.tap, input.hold), and read through\n// game.debug.snapshot(). Run it: node game/test.mjs (exit code 1 on a failure). Write one like it for your game.\nimport vm from 'node:vm';\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n\nconst ctx = { console };\nvm.createContext(ctx);\nfor (const f of ['../my-3d2dge.js', './game.js']) vm.runInContext(readFileSync(new URL(f, import.meta.url), 'utf8'), ctx, { filename: f });\nconst E = ctx.My3D2dge, game = E.current, I = game.input, snap = () => game.debug.snapshot();\nlet failed = 0;\nconst check = (what, ok, detail = '') => { console.log((ok ? 'ok   ' : 'FAIL ') + what + (ok ? '' : ': ' + detail)); if (!ok) failed++; };\n\ngame.advance(.5);\ncheck('the title waits for start', snap().scene === 'title', JSON.stringify(snap()));\nI.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\ncheck('start begins play', snap().scene === 'play', JSON.stringify(snap()));\n\n// climb: stand facing the roof's west wall (cells 7-9 of rows 4-6: x 112-160, y 64-112), press jump to take hold, then\n// climb with up (up the screen is up the wall) until it pulls up onto the top\ngame.debug.teleport(100, 88, 0, 0);\nI.tap('jump'); game.advance(.1);\ncheck('jump next to a wall takes hold of it', snap().climbing, JSON.stringify(snap()));\nI.hold('up'); game.advance(.6); I.hold('up', false); game.advance(1);\ncheck('climbing up pulls up onto the roof', !snap().climbing && snap().hero[2] === 32, JSON.stringify(snap()));\n\n// take the gem (on the roof's middle cell), then hop down into the street with it\ngame.debug.teleport(136, 88, 32, 0); game.advance(.1);\ncheck('walking onto the gem takes it', snap().got, JSON.stringify(snap()));\ngame.debug.teleport(156, 120, 32, Math.PI / 2); I.hold('down'); game.advance(1.5); I.hold('down', false); game.advance(.5);\ncheck('back in the street with the gem wins', snap().scene === 'end' && snap().won, JSON.stringify(snap()));\n\n// get seen: stand in front of the guard, in the open, until its alert fills\nI.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\nconst [gx, gy, gf] = snap().guard;\ngame.debug.teleport(gx + Math.cos(gf) * 40, gy + Math.sin(gf) * 40, 0, gf + Math.PI);\ngame.advance(2.5);\ncheck('the guard catches a hero it sees', snap().scene === 'end' && !snap().won, JSON.stringify(snap()));\ncheck('no errors', !game.errors.length, game.errors.map(e => e.message).join(' | '));\n\nconsole.log(failed ? `${failed} check(s) failed` : 'all checks passed');\nprocess.exit(failed ? 1 : 0);","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KyvR2yUVSsEKq1WedZAZjC","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
