{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0176ShaJD3y6waB58pnaBjjd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Build plan: an original surgical action game inspired by *Trauma Center: Second Opinion*\n2\t\n3\t**You build this game from this plan, alone.** Nobody can answer questions. Where the plan leaves a detail open, decide and keep going.\n4\t\n5\t## Contents\n6\t\n7\t0. How to use this plan\n8\t1. The game on one page\n9\t2. Direction decisions\n10\t3. Verified engine and Hub facts\n11\t4. Game flow\n12\t5. The operation\n13\t6. Controls and prompts\n14\t7. Things on the table\n15\t8. HUSH: the strains\n16\t9. The cases\n17\t10. Story bible\n18\t11. The ward\n19\t12. Multiplayer: Grand Rounds\n20\t13. Attract mode\n21\t14. Dev mode\n22\t15. UI and the 10-foot rules\n23\t16. Art direction\n24\t17. Animation\n25\t18. Audio\n26\t19. Code architecture\n27\t20. Testing\n28\t21. Build order\n29\t22. Done checklist (TASK.md, line by line)\n30\t- Appendix A: tissue painter\n31\t- Appendix B: a site recipe\n32\t- Appendix C: portrait painter\n33\t- Appendix D: Hub skeleton, page head and harness runner\n34\t- Appendix E: gamepad stub and touch-notice patch\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## 0. How to use this plan\n39\t\n40\t- **Authority.** TASK.md is the brief. It wins on whatever it states: file and folder names, packaging, environment rules, the exit interview. This plan decides what TASK.md leaves open: genre, perspective, design, story, content, structure, build order. The engine's own docs win on API details.\n41\t- **Checked against** my-3D2dge v0.88.0 and the AI Hub client 1.4.0 on this computer. Both repositories move fast. Items marked *Verified* were run here. If your clone's docs disagree, trust your clone and adapt.\n42\t- **Words.** \"The game\" is the game: you pick its title. \"The HTML file\" is the single deliverable TASK.md names. \"The exit interview\" is the JSON file TASK.md describes. Every path in this plan is relative to your dev folder.\n43\t- **First job: the title.** Follow TASK.md's title step exactly, real random number generator included, because your folder and file names depend on it. Keep the result in one constant, `GAME_TITLE` in `src/config.js`, and read it everywhere (title screen, page `<title>`, About, Hub card). Nothing in the story depends on it, so any title works. The in-fiction names below (the hospital, the disease) are not the title.\n44\t- **Reference code.** Appendices A to E ran on this computer. Copy them. They set the quality floor for the picture and the multiplayer; improve on them freely.\n45\t- **Keep a log from day one.** `notes/feedback.md`: every issue a test catches (and whether it would have been game breaking), every engine surprise, every prompt confusion. You need real numbers and real answers for the Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback at the end.\n46\t\n47\t---\n48\t\n49\t## 1. The game on one page\n50\t\n51\t**Pitch.** You are a second-year surgical resident on the night shift at a rain-soaked harbor hospital, and the only doctor who can *hear* the disease. Each case is a frantic operation at the table: call for the right tool, cut on the line, drain the blood, stitch, keep the pulse alive, with a deadpan nurse at your elbow. Between cases it is a full-blooded hospital soap opera. The routine cases turn strange: patients who feel no pain, smiling while they die, and glowing parasites under the tissue that sing to each other.\n52\t\n53\t**Core loop.** Read the field, call the right tool, work fast and clean, keep the pulse alive, close, take your grade.\n54\t\n55\t**Pillars.**\n56\t1. **Hands first.** Every tool is a different gesture with its own sound, rumble and picture. A player on a couch with a DualSense, 10 feet from a TV, reads the field at a glance.\n57\t2. **Melodrama played straight.** Soap-opera stakes, medical jargon, big sincere speeches, and a nurse who has seen it all.\n58\t3. **Every case adds one new idea.** No operation repeats another with bigger numbers.\n59\t4. **Hard but fair.** The tension is real, and a failure costs seconds of replay, not the whole case.\n60\t\n61\t**What carries over from the source, and what gets fixed.** (From Wikipedia and Marc Normandin's Retro XP essay \"Re-release this: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\", read for this plan. Extra research is optional.)\n62\t\n63\t| The source does this | This game keeps it as | The source's known flaw | This game's fix |\n64\t|---|---|---|---|\n65\t| One hand picks a tool from an 8-way stick wheel, the other aims it | Left stick wheel, right stick or mouse cursor | Pointer precision is hard to carry to a pad | Cursor assist, guide-following, fine mode (5.3) |\n66\t| Time limit, vitals, graded on time and precision, S rank | The same frame with its own grades | Difficulty spikes | Phase checkpoints, assist, offered easing (5.10) |\n67\t| A supernatural surgeon's ability, once per operation, drawn as a gesture | A gauge you earn by clean work | The gesture was unreliable | One button, earned and rechargeable (5.7) |\n68\t| Two doctors with different gifts | Two doctors, then both at once in the finale | The second doctor was mostly post-game | She joins the main story (Chapter 3) |\n69\t| Parasite strains fought as boss battles | HUSH strains, each with its own rules | Recycled content | Every strain and case changes the rules |\n70\t| Melodramatic plot, expressive portraits | Same register, new story and cast | \"Emotional disconnect\" | Patients are people, and the ward shows them afterward |\n71\t| 4:3, single player | | No widescreen, no multiplayer | Any screen shape, and 1 to 4 player co-op |\n72\t\n73\t**Originality rule (hard line).** TASK.md asks for an original game. Use the genre and the kinds of mechanics; invent everything else. Never use these or close variants, in play or in text: Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Robert Hoffman, Kenneth Blackwell, Hope Hospital, Angeles Bay, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT and its strain names, the Healing Touch. Do not reproduce its art, music, levels or operations. The source's name appears only as TASK.md allows: the riff in the title, and the Source line of the About screen.\n74\t\n75\t**Content note.** Stylized and tasteful, about E10+: pixel-art tissue, no lingering gore, unconscious patients, jokes about the job and never at a patient's expense. A Settings option recolors blood (5.11).\n76\t\n77\t---\n78\t\n79\t## 2. Direction decisions\n80\t\n81\tSettled. Do not reopen them unless the engine forces you to.\n82\t\n83\t| Topic | Decision |\n84\t|---|---|\n85\t| **Genre** | Surgical action game, with story scenes between operations and a small walkable ward |\n86\t| **Perspective** | Surgery: first-person top-down on the operative field, using the `overhead` view. Story: front-on portraits on painted stages. Ward and short cinematics: `threequarter` view. Title: `side` view of the hospital at night |\n87\t| **Tech** | The full engine, `my-3d2dge.js` from the handoff package (it has every module, including `story`). Plain JavaScript, no framework, no build beyond your own bundler, no WebGPU, no 3D add-on, no CDN: the page must work from `file://` and inside the Hub with no network |\n88\t| **Resolution** | `res: [426, 240], expand: true`. See section 3 for why |\n89\t| **Art** | The engine's pixel look at PS1 level: hard-edged shading through hand-picked ramps, ordered dithering. New art by code for everything surgical; asset store only for generic props |\n90\t| **Animation** | The engine's rigs and continuous-value animation for bodies. Procedural animation for organs, parasites and instruments |\n91\t| **Audio** | Everything synthesized in code, no audio files. Gibberish voices. Music slider defaults to 10% |\n92\t| **Online** | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `shared-world` mode, 1 to 4 players. On its own: couch co-op, same mode (section 12) |\n93\t| **Package** | One self-contained HTML file with everything inlined, as TASK.md names it |\n94\t| **Research** | Done for this plan (section 1). More is optional, your choice. Report what you actually did in the Research field |\n95\t| **Performance** | Unlimited, as TASK.md says: no FPS targets, no optimization passes. Spend it on detail. *Verified:* the tissue shader takes 3 to 5 ms a frame at 480x270 under software rendering, so there is plenty of room |\n96\t| **Effort** | Yours to spend. Spend it first on how the hands feel, then on the writing, then on everything else |\n97\t\n98\t---\n99\t\n100\t## 3. Verified engine and Hub facts\n101\t\n102\tAll of these ran on this computer, so you can rely on them and skip rediscovering them.\n103\t\n104\t1. **Getting the code.** This is 16 MB, not the whole repository:\n105\t   ```sh\n106\t   git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/my-3d2dge\n107\t   git -C vendor/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n108\t   mkdir -p vendor/hub && cd vendor/hub && for f in README.md session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js examples/arena.html; do curl -fsSL --create-dirs -o \"$f\" \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f\"; done\n109\t   ```\n110\t   Read `vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/README.md`, then its `API.md` whole, then its `docs/ASSETS.md`. Grep its `API-INDEX.md` for names. Read `vendor/hub/README.md` whole: it is the protocol. The engine's tool paths below (`tools/...`, `kits/...`, `docs/...`) are inside `vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/`.\n111\t2. **Screen sizes.** `res: [426, 240], expand: true` gives these `r.W x r.H`: 1920x1080 → 480x270 (4x), 1280x720 → 427x240 (3x), 1366x768 → 456x256 (3x), 2560x1440 → 427x240 (6x), 3840x2160 → 427x240 (9x), 3440x1440 → 574x240, 1024x768 → 512x384, iPhone 13 upright → 426x726, iPad upright → 426x568. So at least 426 x 240 always shows, on every shape. The 5x7 font at scale 1 is 21 px tall at 720p and 28 px at 1080p: readable from 10 feet. `res: 'ps1'` gives only 320 wide on an upright phone, and `res: [480, 270]` falls to 2x at 720p, so use the numbers above.\n112\t3. **Input map.** A custom map must keep the moves and menu actions or the engine warns and menus break. Start from `const { attack, dash, skill, jump, ...base } = E.Input.DEFAULT;`.\n113\t4. **Music default.** `menu: { settings: [{ key: 'music', value: .1 }] }` makes `game.settings.music === .1` and `game.audio.musicVolume === .1` on a fresh start.\n114\t5. **Glyphs.** `{ key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']] }` in the same list gives the required Settings option. With `useTool: ['Pad7', 'Pad0', 'Mouse0', 'Space']`, `input.label('useTool')` gave `R2` (PlayStation), `RT` (Xbox), `Left click` (mouse last used), `Space` (keyboard last used). The Pad code listed first is the one shown. The 5x7 font draws `✕ ○ □ △`.\n115\t6. **Pad reading** with the stub in Appendix E: `input.padIndex === 0`, `input.padStyle() === 'playstation'`, `input.value('useTool') === .5` from `buttons[7].value = .5`, `input.padAim` is the right stick and `input.padMove` the left, both after the dead zone.\n116\t7. **Mouse.** `input.pointer()` is in the HUD's screen pixels (mouse at 300,300 on a 1920 window gave [75,75]). Moving the mouse sets `input.lastDevice = 'mouse'`.\n117\t8. **Draw order for the operation.** Call `game.focus(game.W / 2, game.H / 2, 0)` every update in the `overhead` view; then `r.w(0, 0, 0)` is `[0, 0]` and world units are screen pixels. In `draw`, put the field on `r.ctx` first (`r.ctx.drawImage(field.cv, o[0], o[1])` with `o = r.w(0, 0, 0)`), so `game.particles` land on top of it and `game.shake` moves it. HUD goes in `r.overlay`. Fill the buffer with drape green first: a shake exposes the edge.\n118\t9. **Engine portraits are not enough.** `rig.drawPortrait` at 64 px and up looks crude, and a `hat: 'cap'` covers the eyes. Use the bust painter in Appendix C for every face. Keep rigs for bodies at game scale.\n119\t10. **Menus at TV size.** The pause and settings menus are the engine's HTML (`.esh`), too small for 10 feet (13 px on a 1080p screen). This CSS fixes them: `html .esh{zoom:var(--ui,1)}html .esh-mod{max-height:none}`, with `--ui` set to `Math.max(1, innerHeight / 540)` on load and on resize. Every pause row fits at 1080p and 720p, and focus scrolls the long Settings screen. Reduce density too: `html .esh-row small{display:none}html .esh-row:focus-within small{display:block}` shows a row's help only on the focused row. Keep the `html` prefix: the engine injects its own CSS at runtime, and the same specificity could lose to it.\n120\t11. **Touch notice.** `new E.Game({ touch: 'never' })` gives no touch controls, drops the Touch settings, and shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" with a Settings button. But the notice covers the screen at 88% black, which hides the attract mode TASK.md wants running. Patch it (Appendix E).\n121\t12. **The Hub works on this engine.** The skeleton in Appendix D passed all four harness scenarios (14 or 15 checks each) and still runs standalone. The options that matter in a session are `live: true`, `autoPause: false`, `corner: false`, `menu.quit: false`.\n122\t13. **Headless draw.** `new E.Game({ draw: true })` in Node runs the tissue painter and the bust painter on the engine's stand-in canvas with no errors, so draw code can be tested without a browser.\n123\t14. **Session storage.** In a session the client replaces storage with an empty in-memory store, so settings fall back to defaults (music 10%) and no save carries in. No code is needed for this.\n124\t15. **Pausing in a session.** With `pausable: true`, `live: true`, `menu.quit: false`, the pause menu shows Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen, and the shared world keeps stepping under it.\n125\t16. **Mouse bindings.** `WheelUp`, `WheelDown`, `Mouse1` (middle) and `Mouse2` (right) work as action codes. A scene with `camera: { zoom: false, wheel: false }` leaves the wheel to the game.\n126\t17. **Idle tracking.** A `game.hook('input', dt => ...)` that checks `input.anyPressed()`, `input.held.size`, `input.clicked()`, a change in `input.mouse.t`, and stick lengths gives a timer that counts up when idle and resets on a mouse move. It runs on every scene and while paused.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t## 4. Game flow\n131\t\n132\t| Scene | What happens | Leaves to |\n133\t|---|---|---|\n134\t| `boot` | The engine's boot steps; bake shared art; in a session, connect to the Hub | `title`, or `hubround` in a session |\n135\t| `title` | Hospital skyline at night in the rain, logo, heart-trace underline, \"Press Start\". 10 s idle → `attract` | `menu` |\n136\t| `menu` | Continue, New Game, Case Files, Grand Rounds, Settings, About. 10 s idle → `attract` | as chosen |\n137\t| `difficulty` | Three big cards: Intern, Resident, Attending, each with one Quill line | `story` |\n138\t| `story` | Portrait stage, backdrop, typed lines with gibberish voice, chapter cards, short cinematics | `chart`, `ward` |\n139\t| `chart` | One clipboard: patient, complaint, first opinion. Quill reads it aloud. Optional Lightbox (9.1) | `op` |\n140\t| `op` | The operation, phase by phase, pausable | `results` or `lost` |\n141\t| `lost` | Flatline, a line from Quill, then Retry from this phase, Restart case, Lower difficulty for this case (after 2 failures), Quit | `op`, `menu` |\n142\t| `results` | \"CASE CLOSED\" stamp, big rank letter, four stat tiles one at a time, Quill's one-liner | `story` |\n143\t| `ward` | Walkable ward between cases: saved patients, staff, door to the next case | `story`, `chart` |\n144\t| `casefiles` | One folder at a time: site art, ranks as three stamps (one per difficulty), replay. Skills Lab | `chart`, `op` |\n145\t| `lobby` | Grand Rounds seats: press to join, add AI colleagues, Start | `op` (Grand Rounds) |\n146\t| `about` | About/Credits, one section per page | `menu` |\n147\t| `credits` | The ending's roll, then a last scene, then `title` | `title` |\n148\t| `attract` | Looping AI demo. Any input → `title` | `title` |\n149\t| `hubround` | Session only: own card, countdown, the Grand Rounds case, no menus | removed by the Hub |\n150\t\n151\tA first launch plays: title → menu → New Game → difficulty → Chapter 1 card → first scene → Case 1, with the first tool in the player's hand inside a minute.\n152\t\n153\t---\n154\t\n155\t## 5. The operation\n156\t\n157\tSim rules for this whole section: fixed steps, `dt` from the engine, all state in one plain object (19.2). Every number below is a default in the `TUNING` table in `src/config.js`, read live so dev mode's sliders reach it.\n158\t\n159\t### 5.1 The table\n160\t\n161\t- Positions are in **field units (u)**: design space 426 x 240, origin top left, 1 u = 1 buffer pixel. To the screen: add `OX = (r.W - 426) >> 1`, `OY = (r.H - 240) >> 1`.\n162\t- The **cavity** is an ellipse centered at (213, 116) with radii (171, 91). Everything interactive stays inside it. Bigger screens show more drape, never a bigger cavity. Its ellipse leaves the four corners free for the HUD.\n163\t- Draw back to front: drape fill, field (tissue, pools, wounds, gel, char, ridges, all through the painter's stamps), objects, engine particles, then `r.overlay` (stitches, strains' glow, guides, instruments, cursors, HUD).\n164\t\n165\t**HUD on the smallest screen, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`:**\n166\t\n167\t| Piece | Place | Contents |\n168\t|---|---|---|\n169\t| Monitor | (6, 6), 148 x 30 | ECG trace, vitals number at font scale 2 |\n170\t| Clock | right edge, y 6, 70 x 22 | `01:42` at font scale 2 |\n171\t| Objective | top center, y 12 | At most 26 characters, a verb first: \"Drain the pool\" |\n172\t| Speaker | bottom left, a 56 x 64 crop of a cached bust (from its 28, 16) at (4, H-62), masked in theatre | Who is barking |\n173\t| Bark box | (62, H-34), 236 x 28 | Name, one line of at most 40 characters, gone after 2.5 s plus .05 s a character |\n174\t| Steady gauge | bottom right, 90 x 16 | Bar, its glyph, READY when usable |\n175\t| Flow | above the gauge | `FLOW x6` from a chain of 3 |\n176\t| Cursor prompt | beside the cursor, flipping near edges | `R2 Pull`, only over a valid target |\n177\t\n178\tKeep it this sparse. The patient is the screen.\n179\t\n180\t### 5.2 Vitals, clock, slips\n181\t\n182\t- **Vitals** run 0 to 99 (a float in the sim, shown whole). Each case has a start value and a base drain. Each open hazard adds its own drain (section 7).\n183\t- The player feels vitals through five channels so no one needs to look: the number, the ECG's rate, the monitor beep's rate and pitch, a double-thump rumble below 30, and Quill's calm readouts (\"Pulse forty. I mention it only in passing.\"). Below 30 the number flashes and CRITICAL appears, so it is never color alone.\n184\t- At 0: Code Blue if unused (5.8), else the patient is lost.\n185\t- **Clock:** counts down in world time. At 0 the patient is lost, with no Code Blue (\"Time of death, oh-four-twelve.\").\n186\t- **Slips** (mistakes): no cap on them. Each one costs vitals, breaks Flow, and shows in Results.\n187\t\n188\t### 5.3 The cursor\n189\t\n190\tThis is where the game lives or dies on a pad. Make it good before making anything else.\n191\t\n192\t- **Pad:** velocity = `stick^1.7 x 330 u/s` with a .06 s ramp. Fine mode (L2) multiplies by .35. Full deflection crosses the field in about a second, and small pushes are slow enough to place a stitch.\n193\t- **Magnet** (`assist` setting, default .6): within 22 u of a target valid for the held tool, speed drops by `.55 x assist` and, while the stick is near neutral, the cursor is pulled to the target at `90 x assist` u/s.\n194\t- **Guide-follow:** during a Scalpel stroke or a stitch, the part of the stick's push across the guide is scaled by `1 - .8 x assist`. At full assist a rough push along the line traces it, which is the accessibility floor.\n195\t- **Mouse:** the cursor is the pointer, 1:1, never moved by the game. Assist only widens the tolerances, by up to 1 u.\n196\t- **Keyboard:** WASD or arrows at 200 u/s, Shift for fine. D-pad nudges at 40 u/s.\n197\t- **Look:** an instrument whose tip is the exact aim point, gripped by a gloved hand down and to the right, so the hand never hides the target. In co-op the glove is the seat's color. Hide the OS pointer in `op`, and bring it back while paused.\n198\t- **Loupe:** holding Fine for .15 s shows a 2x lens of radius 34 around the cursor, pixel-doubled from the finished frame, with row-by-row `drawImage` and smoothing off. It is cosmetic.\n199\t\n200\t### 5.4 The tool wheel\n201\t\n202\tEight tools, eight stick directions, always the same place, so hands learn them like a real surgeon's \"Forceps!\".\n203\t\n204\t```\n205\t         N  Forceps\n206\t  NW Sonar        NE Scalpel\n207\tW Suction            E Suture\n208\t  SW Cautery      SE Salve\n209\t         S  Syringe\n210\t```\n211\t\n212\tTop takes, right closes, bottom medicates, left clears. Number keys 1 to 8 run clockwise from Forceps.\n213\t\n214\t- **Pad:** push the left stick past .6. The sector stable for 80 ms is picked. The ring shows around the cursor while the stick is out and .3 s after, with a clack, Quill's slap in the palm, and his gibberish bark of the tool's name. L1 and R1 step through the wheel too.\n215\t- **Mouse:** scroll steps. Hold Right click, flick, release to pick.\n216\t- **Keys:** 1 to 8, or Q and E.\n217\t- A swap takes .12 s. Time does not stop for the wheel.\n218\t- **Hints:** on Intern (and with Hints on, after 6 s of idling or a repeated slip), the next tool's tile glows and Quill names it.\n219\t- Tile art is 20 x 20, each tool a distinct silhouette, readable at 10 feet.\n220\t\n221\t### 5.5 The eight tools\n222\t\n223\tAnalog input only ever gives more control, never more power: a key or a click counts as a full press. \"Hold or toggle\" for sustained tools is a setting.\n224\t\n225\t| Tool | Gesture | Marker on the field |\n226\t|---|---|---|\n227\t| **Forceps** | Grip, pull along the axis, release | White corner brackets |\n228\t| **Scalpel** | Press on the start dot, trace the line | Purple dashed marker line, start dot |\n229\t| **Suture** | Press at one side, cross the wound, release at the other | Blue paired dots |\n230\t| **Salve** | Hold and paint over the patch | Green hatch |\n231\t| **Syringe** | Hold to draw, release to inject | A plus-sign ring on targets. Anywhere else goes to the IV |\n232\t| **Cautery** | Hold the beam on the target | Orange ring |\n233\t| **Suction** | Hold over the pool | None needed, the pool is the marker |\n234\t| **Sonar** | Hold to sweep a lens | Arcs when something hidden is near |\n235\t\n236\t**Forceps.** Grip within 12 u at trigger .3. An *embedded* object has an out-axis and a depth: progress is cursor travel along the axis. Pulling faster than 120 u/s, or more than 8 u off axis, **tears** (laceration, vitals -4, progress -30%). Past its depth it comes free and follows the cursor. Release 24 u or more from its origin, or past the cavity's edge, and Quill's dish catches it with a clink. Grade: no tears and under par is Textbook, one tear is Clean, else Sloppy. A held piece can be handed off in some cases: release inside a **hold zone** and Quill clamps it (Case 5).\n237\t\n238\t**Scalpel.** The guide is a polyline. Press within 8 u of the start. Progress is the furthest arc length reached. Distance from the line over 9 u for .12 s is a **slip** (short laceration, vitals -3), and the stroke goes on when you come back. Release early and resume within 6 u. Grade by mean distance: 1.8 u or less is Textbook, 4 u or less is Clean, else Sloppy. Variants: a *ring* (closed loop around a mass), and a *slash* (any stroke crossing a target at 60 u/s or more). Dragging the blade 6 u over healthy tissue with no guide is a slip. Crossing a nerve or eloquent line costs vitals -12 and always counts as a slip.\n239\t\n240\t**Suture.** A wound has N stitches, each a pair of points across it. Press within 9 u of the next near point, carry across, release within 6 u of the far point. A bad release is a loose stitch (vitals -1, redo it). Each stitch set closes 1/N of the wound and cuts its bleeding. Per stitch: .6 s or less is Textbook, 1.2 s or less is Clean. The wound's grade is the mean. The needle arcs across, the thread pulls tight with a pop, and the wound's lips close on the field.\n241\t\n242\t**Salve.** Paints a coverage grid of 4 u cells with a 10 u brush. A patch needs 90% coverage, then heals with a sparkle and gives +2 vitals. It also coats some strain parts. It cannot harm, only waste time.\n243\t\n244\t**Syringe.** Hold to fill, 0 to full in .55 s. Release to inject. Over a target within 10 u, that is a targeted dose: at .9 full or more it counts Textbook; held .3 s past full gets an air bubble (Sloppy, half effect). Anywhere else it goes to the IV: vitals +18 x fill (Intern +24, Attending +12). .35 s cooldown. This is the panic button, so it must always feel available.\n245\t\n246\t**Cautery.** Hold for a beam on a 6 u spot. Polyp .5 s, pinhole bleeder .35 s, Staccato bead .12 s. Heat fills at .5/s and cools at .6/s; at 1 it locks for 1.2 s. On bare tissue for .25 s it leaves char, -.8 vitals/s, and a slip at .6 s. Grade: no char and no lockout during the kill is Textbook.\n247\t\n248\t**Suction.** Hold within 14 u of a pool: removes 60 volume/s, with a gurgle that rises as it empties. Objects under a pool can't be seen or targeted until it's cleared. It has no grade, but it never breaks Flow, so it is always a safe move.\n249\t\n250\t**Sonar.** Hold for a lens of radius 38 in the cold palette (the painter's `lens`). Hidden objects show inside it and stay marked for 4 s after it leaves. A ping rises in rate with proximity. HUSH's song grows and pans toward the nearest strain, so the player can hunt by ear. Hidden objects cannot be touched until marked.\n251\t\n252\t### 5.6 Grades, Flow, ranks\n253\t\n254\t- Every finished action gets a popup at its location, with sound: **Sloppy** (x.4), **Clean** (x1), **Textbook** (x1.5). Text, size and sound differ, so it is never color alone.\n255\t- **Flow:** consecutive non-Sloppy actions, +5% each up to x2. A slip resets it.\n256\t- **End bonus:** clock left x 2, vitals x 3.\n257\t- **Rank:** score against the case's `par` (a perfect bot's score at Resident, section 20): S 95%, A 80%, B 60%, C 40%, else D. On Attending, S with no slips earns a star, **★ Textbook**. Each difficulty keeps its own best rank.\n258\t- **Results shows only four tiles**, one at a time, each with a tick: Time left, Pulse held, Technique (share of Clean or better), Best Flow. Then the rank stamp and Quill's line for that rank. No tables.\n259\t\n260\t### 5.7 The Steady (the gift)\n261\t\n262\tAn old word in theatre for surgeons who can do what shouldn't be possible. The gauge runs 0 to 100, shared in the finale: +5 per Clean, +10 per Textbook, +.5/s. One button (△, F or Middle click). Activation never fails.\n263\t\n264\t**Long Breath (Dr. Vale).** Needs 35. The world runs at .25 speed while the hands stay at full speed. Drains 16/s; press again to stop. Implement it with a world-time factor in the sim, **not** `game.timeScale`. The field shifts to the cold palette (the painter's `slow`), so glowing strains and dark blood stand out. Music thins, sound low-passes, the heartbeat dominates. Strains and hazards all pulse with outlines. This image is the game's signature: *Verified*, it reads beautifully in the prototype.\n265\t\n266\t**Second Look (Dr. Sokolova).** Needs 45. Rewinds the sim up to 6 s. A ring buffer holds a snapshot of the state every .1 s. Restoring is exact: vitals, clock, objects, RNG. Then, for the next 6 s, **ghosts** replay: pale afterimages of her own cursor and of every strain, taken from the buffer, where they were last time. Rewinding is literally a second look at what is about to happen. Presentation: 1.2 s of fast reverse with VCR tracking lines, a falling tape sweep, Quill's bark reversed. Her line: \"No. Again.\" The rewound seconds' grades and score vanish. There is no rank penalty. Not available in Grand Rounds.\n267\t\n268\t**Consult (Cases 10 and 11).** □ or C hands over to the other doctor in .4 s. Gauge shared, each doctor's gift and passive apply: Vale has +15% cursor speed and +25% Sonar radius; Sokolova has +.2 assist and +1 u suture tolerance.\n269\t\n270\t### 5.8 Code Blue\n271\t\n272\tThe first time vitals reach 0 in a case (not on Attending): the world freezes and the paddles come out. The ECG scrolls full-width with 4 charge pips, a marker crossing the peak every .9 s. Press L2 and R2 together (Left click or Space on keyboard and mouse) inside ±.14 s of the peak; ±.06 s is Textbook. Two hits in four attempts: vitals to 30, and play resumes with a jolt, a huge rumble and a white flash. Fewer: patient lost. \"Clear!\" in gibberish. Grade counts against rank only by what the time cost.\n273\t\n274\t### 5.9 Closing\n275\t\n276\tWhen the last phase clears, the op doesn't just end. Quill: \"Dressing.\" A timing ring closes on the wound; press on the ring for a stamp, with a bonus for the timing. \"CASE CLOSED\" stamps across the screen, with `TIME OF COMPLETION 02:14`. Sound drops to one held chord. This is the reward beat. Land it.\n277\t\n278\t### 5.10 Failing and retrying\n279\t\n280\t- Phase boundaries are checkpoints. **Retry from this phase** restores the phase's starting state with rank capped at B. **Restart case** is uncapped.\n281\t- After 2 failures in one phase, Quill offers Intern for this case only.\n282\t- Tutorial moments (Cases 1 to 4) freeze the clock and vitals until the first success of a new verb, with a big prompt beside the target.\n283\t\n284\t**Difficulty.** Intern: drains x.7, clock x1.3, hints on, assist +.2. Resident: baseline. Attending: drains x1.3, clock x.8, no Code Blue, hints only after 10 s, ★ available. Changeable between cases.\n285\t\n286\t### 5.11 Feel table\n287\t\n288\tEvery tool gets all three channels. `input.rumble(strength, seconds)`, scaled by the engine's Vibration setting.\n289\t\n290\t| Moment | Sound | Particles or picture | Rumble |\n291\t|---|---|---|---|\n292\t| Tool swap | Clack, palm slap | Ring flash, instrument changes | .15 / .03 |\n293\t| Forceps grip, free | Tick, then clink in dish | Glint, droplets | .15 / .04, .3 / .05 |\n294\t| Tear | Wet rip | Spurt, hit-stop .04 s, screen shake 2 | .5 / .1 |\n295\t| Scalpel stroke | Rising hiss | Thin blood line opening behind the tip | .1 steady |\n296\t| Stitch pop | Thread twang | Lips close, thread pulls tight | .25 / .05 |\n297\t| Cautery | Sizzle | Spark, smoke, hot spot | .2 steady |\n298\t| Suction | Gurgle rising in pitch | Pool shrinks toward the tip | .1 steady |\n299\t| Syringe | Plunger hiss | Bubbles in the barrel, pulse flash on the monitor | .1 / .1 |\n300\t| Textbook | Bright two-note chime | Big popup, gold sparkle | .2 / .05 |\n301\t| Slip | Low thud, Quill sighs | Red flash on the field, monitor jumps | .6 / .12 |\n302\t| Vitals critical | Monitor alarm | Vignette, number flashes | double thump per beat |\n303\t| Code Blue shock | Whine, bang | White flash, field jumps | 1 / .35 |\n304\t| Boss phase | Choir swell | Shake, field darkens | .7 / .4 |\n305\t\n306\tComfort: `game.shake` and `game.flash` already follow the comfort settings. Settings adds **Blood color** (Surgical red, or Clinical teal-grey, a swap of the blood and wound ramps).\n307\t\n308\t---\n309\t\n310\t## 6. Controls and prompts\n311\t\n312\tThe keyboard and mouse are native here, not stand-ins: the mouse is the best pointer in the game, and the pad is the best-feeling couch device.\n313\t\n314\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Keyboard and mouse |\n315\t|---|---|---|\n316\t| Aim | Right stick. D-pad nudges | Mouse, or WASD or arrows |\n317\t| Use tool | R2 (analog) or ✕ | Left click or Space |\n318\t| Fine mode, loupe | L2 | Shift |\n319\t| Pick tool | Left stick flick | 1 to 8. Hold Right click and flick |\n320\t| Step tool | L1, R1 | Q, E, or the wheel |\n321\t| The Steady | △ | F or Middle click |\n322\t| Consult | □ | C |\n323\t| Drop, cancel stroke | ○ | X or Backspace |\n324\t| Code Blue shock | L2 + R2 | Left click or Space |\n325\t| Pause | Options | Esc or P |\n326\t| Ward: walk | Left stick or D-pad | WASD or arrows, or click to walk |\n327\t| Ward: talk, use | ✕ | E or Enter, or click the person |\n328\t| Story: advance | ✕ | Enter, Space or click |\n329\t| Story: fast-forward | Hold □ | Hold Ctrl |\n330\t| Story: backlog | △ | B |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3415d082-194f-4b08-a98a-256652a62b17","uuid":"a9baad30-e1ca-48ae-b82e-f1dd5f328a98","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:02:46.056Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211908__x0e7d30/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Build plan: an original surgical action game inspired by *Trauma Center: Second Opinion*\n\n**You build this game from this plan, alone.** Nobody can answer questions. Where the plan leaves a detail open, decide and keep going.\n\n## Contents\n\n0. How to use this plan\n1. The game on one page\n2. Direction decisions\n3. Verified engine and Hub facts\n4. Game flow\n5. The operation\n6. Controls and prompts\n7. Things on the table\n8. HUSH: the strains\n9. The cases\n10. Story bible\n11. The ward\n12. Multiplayer: Grand Rounds\n13. Attract mode\n14. Dev mode\n15. UI and the 10-foot rules\n16. Art direction\n17. Animation\n18. Audio\n19. Code architecture\n20. Testing\n21. Build order\n22. Done checklist (TASK.md, line by line)\n- Appendix A: tissue painter\n- Appendix B: a site recipe\n- Appendix C: portrait painter\n- Appendix D: Hub skeleton, page head and harness runner\n- Appendix E: gamepad stub and touch-notice patch\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **Authority.** TASK.md is the brief. It wins on whatever it states: file and folder names, packaging, environment rules, the exit interview. This plan decides what TASK.md leaves open: genre, perspective, design, story, content, structure, build order. The engine's own docs win on API details.\n- **Checked against** my-3D2dge v0.88.0 and the AI Hub client 1.4.0 on this computer. Both repositories move fast. Items marked *Verified* were run here. If your clone's docs disagree, trust your clone and adapt.\n- **Words.** \"The game\" is the game: you pick its title. \"The HTML file\" is the single deliverable TASK.md names. \"The exit interview\" is the JSON file TASK.md describes. Every path in this plan is relative to your dev folder.\n- **First job: the title.** Follow TASK.md's title step exactly, real random number generator included, because your folder and file names depend on it. Keep the result in one constant, `GAME_TITLE` in `src/config.js`, and read it everywhere (title screen, page `<title>`, About, Hub card). Nothing in the story depends on it, so any title works. The in-fiction names below (the hospital, the disease) are not the title.\n- **Reference code.** Appendices A to E ran on this computer. Copy them. They set the quality floor for the picture and the multiplayer; improve on them freely.\n- **Keep a log from day one.** `notes/feedback.md`: every issue a test catches (and whether it would have been game breaking), every engine surprise, every prompt confusion. You need real numbers and real answers for the Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback at the end.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game on one page\n\n**Pitch.** You are a second-year surgical resident on the night shift at a rain-soaked harbor hospital, and the only doctor who can *hear* the disease. Each case is a frantic operation at the table: call for the right tool, cut on the line, drain the blood, stitch, keep the pulse alive, with a deadpan nurse at your elbow. Between cases it is a full-blooded hospital soap opera. The routine cases turn strange: patients who feel no pain, smiling while they die, and glowing parasites under the tissue that sing to each other.\n\n**Core loop.** Read the field, call the right tool, work fast and clean, keep the pulse alive, close, take your grade.\n\n**Pillars.**\n1. **Hands first.** Every tool is a different gesture with its own sound, rumble and picture. A player on a couch with a DualSense, 10 feet from a TV, reads the field at a glance.\n2. **Melodrama played straight.** Soap-opera stakes, medical jargon, big sincere speeches, and a nurse who has seen it all.\n3. **Every case adds one new idea.** No operation repeats another with bigger numbers.\n4. **Hard but fair.** The tension is real, and a failure costs seconds of replay, not the whole case.\n\n**What carries over from the source, and what gets fixed.** (From Wikipedia and Marc Normandin's Retro XP essay \"Re-release this: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\", read for this plan. Extra research is optional.)\n\n| The source does this | This game keeps it as | The source's known flaw | This game's fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| One hand picks a tool from an 8-way stick wheel, the other aims it | Left stick wheel, right stick or mouse cursor | Pointer precision is hard to carry to a pad | Cursor assist, guide-following, fine mode (5.3) |\n| Time limit, vitals, graded on time and precision, S rank | The same frame with its own grades | Difficulty spikes | Phase checkpoints, assist, offered easing (5.10) |\n| A supernatural surgeon's ability, once per operation, drawn as a gesture | A gauge you earn by clean work | The gesture was unreliable | One button, earned and rechargeable (5.7) |\n| Two doctors with different gifts | Two doctors, then both at once in the finale | The second doctor was mostly post-game | She joins the main story (Chapter 3) |\n| Parasite strains fought as boss battles | HUSH strains, each with its own rules | Recycled content | Every strain and case changes the rules |\n| Melodramatic plot, expressive portraits | Same register, new story and cast | \"Emotional disconnect\" | Patients are people, and the ward shows them afterward |\n| 4:3, single player | | No widescreen, no multiplayer | Any screen shape, and 1 to 4 player co-op |\n\n**Originality rule (hard line).** TASK.md asks for an original game. Use the genre and the kinds of mechanics; invent everything else. Never use these or close variants, in play or in text: Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Robert Hoffman, Kenneth Blackwell, Hope Hospital, Angeles Bay, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT and its strain names, the Healing Touch. Do not reproduce its art, music, levels or operations. The source's name appears only as TASK.md allows: the riff in the title, and the Source line of the About screen.\n\n**Content note.** Stylized and tasteful, about E10+: pixel-art tissue, no lingering gore, unconscious patients, jokes about the job and never at a patient's expense. A Settings option recolors blood (5.11).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Direction decisions\n\nSettled. Do not reopen them unless the engine forces you to.\n\n| Topic | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| **Genre** | Surgical action game, with story scenes between operations and a small walkable ward |\n| **Perspective** | Surgery: first-person top-down on the operative field, using the `overhead` view. Story: front-on portraits on painted stages. Ward and short cinematics: `threequarter` view. Title: `side` view of the hospital at night |\n| **Tech** | The full engine, `my-3d2dge.js` from the handoff package (it has every module, including `story`). Plain JavaScript, no framework, no build beyond your own bundler, no WebGPU, no 3D add-on, no CDN: the page must work from `file://` and inside the Hub with no network |\n| **Resolution** | `res: [426, 240], expand: true`. See section 3 for why |\n| **Art** | The engine's pixel look at PS1 level: hard-edged shading through hand-picked ramps, ordered dithering. New art by code for everything surgical; asset store only for generic props |\n| **Animation** | The engine's rigs and continuous-value animation for bodies. Procedural animation for organs, parasites and instruments |\n| **Audio** | Everything synthesized in code, no audio files. Gibberish voices. Music slider defaults to 10% |\n| **Online** | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `shared-world` mode, 1 to 4 players. On its own: couch co-op, same mode (section 12) |\n| **Package** | One self-contained HTML file with everything inlined, as TASK.md names it |\n| **Research** | Done for this plan (section 1). More is optional, your choice. Report what you actually did in the Research field |\n| **Performance** | Unlimited, as TASK.md says: no FPS targets, no optimization passes. Spend it on detail. *Verified:* the tissue shader takes 3 to 5 ms a frame at 480x270 under software rendering, so there is plenty of room |\n| **Effort** | Yours to spend. Spend it first on how the hands feel, then on the writing, then on everything else |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Verified engine and Hub facts\n\nAll of these ran on this computer, so you can rely on them and skip rediscovering them.\n\n1. **Getting the code.** This is 16 MB, not the whole repository:\n   ```sh\n   git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/my-3d2dge\n   git -C vendor/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n   mkdir -p vendor/hub && cd vendor/hub && for f in README.md session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js examples/arena.html; do curl -fsSL --create-dirs -o \"$f\" \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f\"; done\n   ```\n   Read `vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/README.md`, then its `API.md` whole, then its `docs/ASSETS.md`. Grep its `API-INDEX.md` for names. Read `vendor/hub/README.md` whole: it is the protocol. The engine's tool paths below (`tools/...`, `kits/...`, `docs/...`) are inside `vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/`.\n2. **Screen sizes.** `res: [426, 240], expand: true` gives these `r.W x r.H`: 1920x1080 → 480x270 (4x), 1280x720 → 427x240 (3x), 1366x768 → 456x256 (3x), 2560x1440 → 427x240 (6x), 3840x2160 → 427x240 (9x), 3440x1440 → 574x240, 1024x768 → 512x384, iPhone 13 upright → 426x726, iPad upright → 426x568. So at least 426 x 240 always shows, on every shape. The 5x7 font at scale 1 is 21 px tall at 720p and 28 px at 1080p: readable from 10 feet. `res: 'ps1'` gives only 320 wide on an upright phone, and `res: [480, 270]` falls to 2x at 720p, so use the numbers above.\n3. **Input map.** A custom map must keep the moves and menu actions or the engine warns and menus break. Start from `const { attack, dash, skill, jump, ...base } = E.Input.DEFAULT;`.\n4. **Music default.** `menu: { settings: [{ key: 'music', value: .1 }] }` makes `game.settings.music === .1` and `game.audio.musicVolume === .1` on a fresh start.\n5. **Glyphs.** `{ key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']] }` in the same list gives the required Settings option. With `useTool: ['Pad7', 'Pad0', 'Mouse0', 'Space']`, `input.label('useTool')` gave `R2` (PlayStation), `RT` (Xbox), `Left click` (mouse last used), `Space` (keyboard last used). The Pad code listed first is the one shown. The 5x7 font draws `✕ ○ □ △`.\n6. **Pad reading** with the stub in Appendix E: `input.padIndex === 0`, `input.padStyle() === 'playstation'`, `input.value('useTool') === .5` from `buttons[7].value = .5`, `input.padAim` is the right stick and `input.padMove` the left, both after the dead zone.\n7. **Mouse.** `input.pointer()` is in the HUD's screen pixels (mouse at 300,300 on a 1920 window gave [75,75]). Moving the mouse sets `input.lastDevice = 'mouse'`.\n8. **Draw order for the operation.** Call `game.focus(game.W / 2, game.H / 2, 0)` every update in the `overhead` view; then `r.w(0, 0, 0)` is `[0, 0]` and world units are screen pixels. In `draw`, put the field on `r.ctx` first (`r.ctx.drawImage(field.cv, o[0], o[1])` with `o = r.w(0, 0, 0)`), so `game.particles` land on top of it and `game.shake` moves it. HUD goes in `r.overlay`. Fill the buffer with drape green first: a shake exposes the edge.\n9. **Engine portraits are not enough.** `rig.drawPortrait` at 64 px and up looks crude, and a `hat: 'cap'` covers the eyes. Use the bust painter in Appendix C for every face. Keep rigs for bodies at game scale.\n10. **Menus at TV size.** The pause and settings menus are the engine's HTML (`.esh`), too small for 10 feet (13 px on a 1080p screen). This CSS fixes them: `html .esh{zoom:var(--ui,1)}html .esh-mod{max-height:none}`, with `--ui` set to `Math.max(1, innerHeight / 540)` on load and on resize. Every pause row fits at 1080p and 720p, and focus scrolls the long Settings screen. Reduce density too: `html .esh-row small{display:none}html .esh-row:focus-within small{display:block}` shows a row's help only on the focused row. Keep the `html` prefix: the engine injects its own CSS at runtime, and the same specificity could lose to it.\n11. **Touch notice.** `new E.Game({ touch: 'never' })` gives no touch controls, drops the Touch settings, and shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" with a Settings button. But the notice covers the screen at 88% black, which hides the attract mode TASK.md wants running. Patch it (Appendix E).\n12. **The Hub works on this engine.** The skeleton in Appendix D passed all four harness scenarios (14 or 15 checks each) and still runs standalone. The options that matter in a session are `live: true`, `autoPause: false`, `corner: false`, `menu.quit: false`.\n13. **Headless draw.** `new E.Game({ draw: true })` in Node runs the tissue painter and the bust painter on the engine's stand-in canvas with no errors, so draw code can be tested without a browser.\n14. **Session storage.** In a session the client replaces storage with an empty in-memory store, so settings fall back to defaults (music 10%) and no save carries in. No code is needed for this.\n15. **Pausing in a session.** With `pausable: true`, `live: true`, `menu.quit: false`, the pause menu shows Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen, and the shared world keeps stepping under it.\n16. **Mouse bindings.** `WheelUp`, `WheelDown`, `Mouse1` (middle) and `Mouse2` (right) work as action codes. A scene with `camera: { zoom: false, wheel: false }` leaves the wheel to the game.\n17. **Idle tracking.** A `game.hook('input', dt => ...)` that checks `input.anyPressed()`, `input.held.size`, `input.clicked()`, a change in `input.mouse.t`, and stick lengths gives a timer that counts up when idle and resets on a mouse move. It runs on every scene and while paused.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Game flow\n\n| Scene | What happens | Leaves to |\n|---|---|---|\n| `boot` | The engine's boot steps; bake shared art; in a session, connect to the Hub | `title`, or `hubround` in a session |\n| `title` | Hospital skyline at night in the rain, logo, heart-trace underline, \"Press Start\". 10 s idle → `attract` | `menu` |\n| `menu` | Continue, New Game, Case Files, Grand Rounds, Settings, About. 10 s idle → `attract` | as chosen |\n| `difficulty` | Three big cards: Intern, Resident, Attending, each with one Quill line | `story` |\n| `story` | Portrait stage, backdrop, typed lines with gibberish voice, chapter cards, short cinematics | `chart`, `ward` |\n| `chart` | One clipboard: patient, complaint, first opinion. Quill reads it aloud. Optional Lightbox (9.1) | `op` |\n| `op` | The operation, phase by phase, pausable | `results` or `lost` |\n| `lost` | Flatline, a line from Quill, then Retry from this phase, Restart case, Lower difficulty for this case (after 2 failures), Quit | `op`, `menu` |\n| `results` | \"CASE CLOSED\" stamp, big rank letter, four stat tiles one at a time, Quill's one-liner | `story` |\n| `ward` | Walkable ward between cases: saved patients, staff, door to the next case | `story`, `chart` |\n| `casefiles` | One folder at a time: site art, ranks as three stamps (one per difficulty), replay. Skills Lab | `chart`, `op` |\n| `lobby` | Grand Rounds seats: press to join, add AI colleagues, Start | `op` (Grand Rounds) |\n| `about` | About/Credits, one section per page | `menu` |\n| `credits` | The ending's roll, then a last scene, then `title` | `title` |\n| `attract` | Looping AI demo. Any input → `title` | `title` |\n| `hubround` | Session only: own card, countdown, the Grand Rounds case, no menus | removed by the Hub |\n\nA first launch plays: title → menu → New Game → difficulty → Chapter 1 card → first scene → Case 1, with the first tool in the player's hand inside a minute.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The operation\n\nSim rules for this whole section: fixed steps, `dt` from the engine, all state in one plain object (19.2). Every number below is a default in the `TUNING` table in `src/config.js`, read live so dev mode's sliders reach it.\n\n### 5.1 The table\n\n- Positions are in **field units (u)**: design space 426 x 240, origin top left, 1 u = 1 buffer pixel. To the screen: add `OX = (r.W - 426) >> 1`, `OY = (r.H - 240) >> 1`.\n- The **cavity** is an ellipse centered at (213, 116) with radii (171, 91). Everything interactive stays inside it. Bigger screens show more drape, never a bigger cavity. Its ellipse leaves the four corners free for the HUD.\n- Draw back to front: drape fill, field (tissue, pools, wounds, gel, char, ridges, all through the painter's stamps), objects, engine particles, then `r.overlay` (stitches, strains' glow, guides, instruments, cursors, HUD).\n\n**HUD on the smallest screen, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`:**\n\n| Piece | Place | Contents |\n|---|---|---|\n| Monitor | (6, 6), 148 x 30 | ECG trace, vitals number at font scale 2 |\n| Clock | right edge, y 6, 70 x 22 | `01:42` at font scale 2 |\n| Objective | top center, y 12 | At most 26 characters, a verb first: \"Drain the pool\" |\n| Speaker | bottom left, a 56 x 64 crop of a cached bust (from its 28, 16) at (4, H-62), masked in theatre | Who is barking |\n| Bark box | (62, H-34), 236 x 28 | Name, one line of at most 40 characters, gone after 2.5 s plus .05 s a character |\n| Steady gauge | bottom right, 90 x 16 | Bar, its glyph, READY when usable |\n| Flow | above the gauge | `FLOW x6` from a chain of 3 |\n| Cursor prompt | beside the cursor, flipping near edges | `R2 Pull`, only over a valid target |\n\nKeep it this sparse. The patient is the screen.\n\n### 5.2 Vitals, clock, slips\n\n- **Vitals** run 0 to 99 (a float in the sim, shown whole). Each case has a start value and a base drain. Each open hazard adds its own drain (section 7).\n- The player feels vitals through five channels so no one needs to look: the number, the ECG's rate, the monitor beep's rate and pitch, a double-thump rumble below 30, and Quill's calm readouts (\"Pulse forty. I mention it only in passing.\"). Below 30 the number flashes and CRITICAL appears, so it is never color alone.\n- At 0: Code Blue if unused (5.8), else the patient is lost.\n- **Clock:** counts down in world time. At 0 the patient is lost, with no Code Blue (\"Time of death, oh-four-twelve.\").\n- **Slips** (mistakes): no cap on them. Each one costs vitals, breaks Flow, and shows in Results.\n\n### 5.3 The cursor\n\nThis is where the game lives or dies on a pad. Make it good before making anything else.\n\n- **Pad:** velocity = `stick^1.7 x 330 u/s` with a .06 s ramp. Fine mode (L2) multiplies by .35. Full deflection crosses the field in about a second, and small pushes are slow enough to place a stitch.\n- **Magnet** (`assist` setting, default .6): within 22 u of a target valid for the held tool, speed drops by `.55 x assist` and, while the stick is near neutral, the cursor is pulled to the target at `90 x assist` u/s.\n- **Guide-follow:** during a Scalpel stroke or a stitch, the part of the stick's push across the guide is scaled by `1 - .8 x assist`. At full assist a rough push along the line traces it, which is the accessibility floor.\n- **Mouse:** the cursor is the pointer, 1:1, never moved by the game. Assist only widens the tolerances, by up to 1 u.\n- **Keyboard:** WASD or arrows at 200 u/s, Shift for fine. D-pad nudges at 40 u/s.\n- **Look:** an instrument whose tip is the exact aim point, gripped by a gloved hand down and to the right, so the hand never hides the target. In co-op the glove is the seat's color. Hide the OS pointer in `op`, and bring it back while paused.\n- **Loupe:** holding Fine for .15 s shows a 2x lens of radius 34 around the cursor, pixel-doubled from the finished frame, with row-by-row `drawImage` and smoothing off. It is cosmetic.\n\n### 5.4 The tool wheel\n\nEight tools, eight stick directions, always the same place, so hands learn them like a real surgeon's \"Forceps!\".\n\n```\n         N  Forceps\n  NW Sonar        NE Scalpel\nW Suction            E Suture\n  SW Cautery      SE Salve\n         S  Syringe\n```\n\nTop takes, right closes, bottom medicates, left clears. Number keys 1 to 8 run clockwise from Forceps.\n\n- **Pad:** push the left stick past .6. The sector stable for 80 ms is picked. The ring shows around the cursor while the stick is out and .3 s after, with a clack, Quill's slap in the palm, and his gibberish bark of the tool's name. L1 and R1 step through the wheel too.\n- **Mouse:** scroll steps. Hold Right click, flick, release to pick.\n- **Keys:** 1 to 8, or Q and E.\n- A swap takes .12 s. Time does not stop for the wheel.\n- **Hints:** on Intern (and with Hints on, after 6 s of idling or a repeated slip), the next tool's tile glows and Quill names it.\n- Tile art is 20 x 20, each tool a distinct silhouette, readable at 10 feet.\n\n### 5.5 The eight tools\n\nAnalog input only ever gives more control, never more power: a key or a click counts as a full press. \"Hold or toggle\" for sustained tools is a setting.\n\n| Tool | Gesture | Marker on the field |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Forceps** | Grip, pull along the axis, release | White corner brackets |\n| **Scalpel** | Press on the start dot, trace the line | Purple dashed marker line, start dot |\n| **Suture** | Press at one side, cross the wound, release at the other | Blue paired dots |\n| **Salve** | Hold and paint over the patch | Green hatch |\n| **Syringe** | Hold to draw, release to inject | A plus-sign ring on targets. Anywhere else goes to the IV |\n| **Cautery** | Hold the beam on the target | Orange ring |\n| **Suction** | Hold over the pool | None needed, the pool is the marker |\n| **Sonar** | Hold to sweep a lens | Arcs when something hidden is near |\n\n**Forceps.** Grip within 12 u at trigger .3. An *embedded* object has an out-axis and a depth: progress is cursor travel along the axis. Pulling faster than 120 u/s, or more than 8 u off axis, **tears** (laceration, vitals -4, progress -30%). Past its depth it comes free and follows the cursor. Release 24 u or more from its origin, or past the cavity's edge, and Quill's dish catches it with a clink. Grade: no tears and under par is Textbook, one tear is Clean, else Sloppy. A held piece can be handed off in some cases: release inside a **hold zone** and Quill clamps it (Case 5).\n\n**Scalpel.** The guide is a polyline. Press within 8 u of the start. Progress is the furthest arc length reached. Distance from the line over 9 u for .12 s is a **slip** (short laceration, vitals -3), and the stroke goes on when you come back. Release early and resume within 6 u. Grade by mean distance: 1.8 u or less is Textbook, 4 u or less is Clean, else Sloppy. Variants: a *ring* (closed loop around a mass), and a *slash* (any stroke crossing a target at 60 u/s or more). Dragging the blade 6 u over healthy tissue with no guide is a slip. Crossing a nerve or eloquent line costs vitals -12 and always counts as a slip.\n\n**Suture.** A wound has N stitches, each a pair of points across it. Press within 9 u of the next near point, carry across, release within 6 u of the far point. A bad release is a loose stitch (vitals -1, redo it). Each stitch set closes 1/N of the wound and cuts its bleeding. Per stitch: .6 s or less is Textbook, 1.2 s or less is Clean. The wound's grade is the mean. The needle arcs across, the thread pulls tight with a pop, and the wound's lips close on the field.\n\n**Salve.** Paints a coverage grid of 4 u cells with a 10 u brush. A patch needs 90% coverage, then heals with a sparkle and gives +2 vitals. It also coats some strain parts. It cannot harm, only waste time.\n\n**Syringe.** Hold to fill, 0 to full in .55 s. Release to inject. Over a target within 10 u, that is a targeted dose: at .9 full or more it counts Textbook; held .3 s past full gets an air bubble (Sloppy, half effect). Anywhere else it goes to the IV: vitals +18 x fill (Intern +24, Attending +12). .35 s cooldown. This is the panic button, so it must always feel available.\n\n**Cautery.** Hold for a beam on a 6 u spot. Polyp .5 s, pinhole bleeder .35 s, Staccato bead .12 s. Heat fills at .5/s and cools at .6/s; at 1 it locks for 1.2 s. On bare tissue for .25 s it leaves char, -.8 vitals/s, and a slip at .6 s. Grade: no char and no lockout during the kill is Textbook.\n\n**Suction.** Hold within 14 u of a pool: removes 60 volume/s, with a gurgle that rises as it empties. Objects under a pool can't be seen or targeted until it's cleared. It has no grade, but it never breaks Flow, so it is always a safe move.\n\n**Sonar.** Hold for a lens of radius 38 in the cold palette (the painter's `lens`). Hidden objects show inside it and stay marked for 4 s after it leaves. A ping rises in rate with proximity. HUSH's song grows and pans toward the nearest strain, so the player can hunt by ear. Hidden objects cannot be touched until marked.\n\n### 5.6 Grades, Flow, ranks\n\n- Every finished action gets a popup at its location, with sound: **Sloppy** (x.4), **Clean** (x1), **Textbook** (x1.5). Text, size and sound differ, so it is never color alone.\n- **Flow:** consecutive non-Sloppy actions, +5% each up to x2. A slip resets it.\n- **End bonus:** clock left x 2, vitals x 3.\n- **Rank:** score against the case's `par` (a perfect bot's score at Resident, section 20): S 95%, A 80%, B 60%, C 40%, else D. On Attending, S with no slips earns a star, **★ Textbook**. Each difficulty keeps its own best rank.\n- **Results shows only four tiles**, one at a time, each with a tick: Time left, Pulse held, Technique (share of Clean or better), Best Flow. Then the rank stamp and Quill's line for that rank. No tables.\n\n### 5.7 The Steady (the gift)\n\nAn old word in theatre for surgeons who can do what shouldn't be possible. The gauge runs 0 to 100, shared in the finale: +5 per Clean, +10 per Textbook, +.5/s. One button (△, F or Middle click). Activation never fails.\n\n**Long Breath (Dr. Vale).** Needs 35. The world runs at .25 speed while the hands stay at full speed. Drains 16/s; press again to stop. Implement it with a world-time factor in the sim, **not** `game.timeScale`. The field shifts to the cold palette (the painter's `slow`), so glowing strains and dark blood stand out. Music thins, sound low-passes, the heartbeat dominates. Strains and hazards all pulse with outlines. This image is the game's signature: *Verified*, it reads beautifully in the prototype.\n\n**Second Look (Dr. Sokolova).** Needs 45. Rewinds the sim up to 6 s. A ring buffer holds a snapshot of the state every .1 s. Restoring is exact: vitals, clock, objects, RNG. Then, for the next 6 s, **ghosts** replay: pale afterimages of her own cursor and of every strain, taken from the buffer, where they were last time. Rewinding is literally a second look at what is about to happen. Presentation: 1.2 s of fast reverse with VCR tracking lines, a falling tape sweep, Quill's bark reversed. Her line: \"No. Again.\" The rewound seconds' grades and score vanish. There is no rank penalty. Not available in Grand Rounds.\n\n**Consult (Cases 10 and 11).** □ or C hands over to the other doctor in .4 s. Gauge shared, each doctor's gift and passive apply: Vale has +15% cursor speed and +25% Sonar radius; Sokolova has +.2 assist and +1 u suture tolerance.\n\n### 5.8 Code Blue\n\nThe first time vitals reach 0 in a case (not on Attending): the world freezes and the paddles come out. The ECG scrolls full-width with 4 charge pips, a marker crossing the peak every .9 s. Press L2 and R2 together (Left click or Space on keyboard and mouse) inside ±.14 s of the peak; ±.06 s is Textbook. Two hits in four attempts: vitals to 30, and play resumes with a jolt, a huge rumble and a white flash. Fewer: patient lost. \"Clear!\" in gibberish. Grade counts against rank only by what the time cost.\n\n### 5.9 Closing\n\nWhen the last phase clears, the op doesn't just end. Quill: \"Dressing.\" A timing ring closes on the wound; press on the ring for a stamp, with a bonus for the timing. \"CASE CLOSED\" stamps across the screen, with `TIME OF COMPLETION 02:14`. Sound drops to one held chord. This is the reward beat. Land it.\n\n### 5.10 Failing and retrying\n\n- Phase boundaries are checkpoints. **Retry from this phase** restores the phase's starting state with rank capped at B. **Restart case** is uncapped.\n- After 2 failures in one phase, Quill offers Intern for this case only.\n- Tutorial moments (Cases 1 to 4) freeze the clock and vitals until the first success of a new verb, with a big prompt beside the target.\n\n**Difficulty.** Intern: drains x.7, clock x1.3, hints on, assist +.2. Resident: baseline. Attending: drains x1.3, clock x.8, no Code Blue, hints only after 10 s, ★ available. Changeable between cases.\n\n### 5.11 Feel table\n\nEvery tool gets all three channels. `input.rumble(strength, seconds)`, scaled by the engine's Vibration setting.\n\n| Moment | Sound | Particles or picture | Rumble |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Tool swap | Clack, palm slap | Ring flash, instrument changes | .15 / .03 |\n| Forceps grip, free | Tick, then clink in dish | Glint, droplets | .15 / .04, .3 / .05 |\n| Tear | Wet rip | Spurt, hit-stop .04 s, screen shake 2 | .5 / .1 |\n| Scalpel stroke | Rising hiss | Thin blood line opening behind the tip | .1 steady |\n| Stitch pop | Thread twang | Lips close, thread pulls tight | .25 / .05 |\n| Cautery | Sizzle | Spark, smoke, hot spot | .2 steady |\n| Suction | Gurgle rising in pitch | Pool shrinks toward the tip | .1 steady |\n| Syringe | Plunger hiss | Bubbles in the barrel, pulse flash on the monitor | .1 / .1 |\n| Textbook | Bright two-note chime | Big popup, gold sparkle | .2 / .05 |\n| Slip | Low thud, Quill sighs | Red flash on the field, monitor jumps | .6 / .12 |\n| Vitals critical | Monitor alarm | Vignette, number flashes | double thump per beat |\n| Code Blue shock | Whine, bang | White flash, field jumps | 1 / .35 |\n| Boss phase | Choir swell | Shake, field darkens | .7 / .4 |\n\nComfort: `game.shake` and `game.flash` already follow the comfort settings. Settings adds **Blood color** (Surgical red, or Clinical teal-grey, a swap of the blood and wound ramps).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Controls and prompts\n\nThe keyboard and mouse are native here, not stand-ins: the mouse is the best pointer in the game, and the pad is the best-feeling couch device.\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Keyboard and mouse |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aim | Right stick. D-pad nudges | Mouse, or WASD or arrows |\n| Use tool | R2 (analog) or ✕ | Left click or Space |\n| Fine mode, loupe | L2 | Shift |\n| Pick tool | Left stick flick | 1 to 8. Hold Right click and flick |\n| Step tool | L1, R1 | Q, E, or the wheel |\n| The Steady | △ | F or Middle click |\n| Consult | □ | C |\n| Drop, cancel stroke | ○ | X or Backspace |\n| Code Blue shock | L2 + R2 | Left click or Space |\n| Pause | Options | Esc or P |\n| Ward: walk | Left stick or D-pad | WASD or arrows, or click to walk |\n| Ward: talk, use | ✕ | E or Enter, or click the person |\n| Story: advance | ✕ | Enter, Space or click |\n| Story: fast-forward | Hold □ | Hold Ctrl |\n| Story: backlog | △ | B |","numLines":330,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1263}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0176ShaJD3y6waB58pnaBjjd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
