{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjCHHYeE2S6s5vrn8pv1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PPXRqQFr2moXkutHP66MKt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211908__x0e7d30/work/PLAN.md","offset":330,"limit":330},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16491,"cache_read_input_tokens":456198,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16491},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3415d082-194f-4b08-a98a-256652a62b17","uuid":"62266b8c-439d-491b-a51b-f326494f0f5c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:02:59.943Z","request_id":"req_011CgjCHH8MAGeeSjUWpc7rJ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PPXRqQFr2moXkutHP66MKt":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211908__x0e7d30/work/PLAN.md","offset":330,"limit":330}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PPXRqQFr2moXkutHP66MKt","type":"tool_result","content":"330\t| Story: backlog | △ | B |\n331\t\n332\tSettings adds **Swap sticks** for left-handers (cursor and wheel trade sticks).\n333\t\n334\t```js\n335\tconst { attack, dash, skill, jump, ...base } = E.Input.DEFAULT;\n336\tconst INPUT = { ...base,\n337\t  useTool: ['Pad7', 'Pad0', 'Mouse0', 'Space'], fine: ['Pad6', 'ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight'], gift: ['Pad3', 'KeyF', 'Mouse1'], consult: ['Pad2', 'KeyC'],\n338\t  toolPrev: ['Pad4', 'KeyQ', 'WheelUp'], toolNext: ['Pad5', 'KeyE', 'WheelDown'], wheelHold: ['Mouse2'],\n339\t  curU: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp'], curD: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown'], curL: ['KeyA', 'ArrowLeft'], curR: ['KeyD', 'ArrowRight'],   // separate from up/down/left/right, which the left stick also presses\n340\t  nudgeU: ['Pad12'], nudgeD: ['Pad13'], nudgeL: ['Pad14'], nudgeR: ['Pad15'],\n341\t  tool1: ['Digit1'], tool2: ['Digit2'], tool3: ['Digit3'], tool4: ['Digit4'], tool5: ['Digit5'], tool6: ['Digit6'], tool7: ['Digit7'], tool8: ['Digit8'],\n342\t  interact: ['Pad0', 'KeyE', 'Enter'], skip: ['Pad2', 'ControlLeft'], log: ['Pad3', 'KeyB'], more: ['Pad3', 'KeyM'] };\n343\t```\n344\t\n345\tRead the two sticks from `input.padAim` and `input.padMove`, the trigger from `input.value('useTool')`. Name the actions and hide `tool1` to `tool8` from rebinding through `menu.controls`. Give the scene `camera: { zoom: false, wheel: false }`, because the wheel is the game's.\n346\t\n347\t**Prompts.** Always through `game.input.label(action)`, so they follow the device used last and the player's rebinding. Draw a label as a button cap: a rounded rectangle with the label in it, the PlayStation or Xbox face colors on face buttons, and plain keycaps for keys. For the sticks, draw a small stick icon with L or R. Never hard-code a key in text. PlayStation glyphs by default with the Xbox option from fact 5.\n348\t\n349\t---\n350\t\n351\t## 7. Things on the table\n352\t\n353\tA registry (`defObject`), each with sim behavior, a draw function kept apart, and a bot plan.\n354\t\n355\t| Type | Looks like | Fixed by | Vitals/s while open | Other effects |\n356\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n357\t| `shard` | Glass or steel sliver with a glint | Forceps along its axis | .1 | |\n358\t| `laceration` | Short red cut | Salve | .2 | Small pool over time |\n359\t| `wound` | Gaping cut with lips | Suture N stitches | .4 | Bleeding falls with each stitch |\n360\t| `bleeder` | Pinhole spurting on the beat | Cautery | .5 | Feeds a pool, 6 volume/s |\n361\t| `arteryTear` | Pulsing spurts | Suture 3 | 1 | Feeds a pool, 14 volume/s |\n362\t| `pool` | Dark wet metaball, specular | Suction | none | Hides what's under it, drifts on a tilt |\n363\t| `polyp` | Pink nub | Cautery | .1 | |\n364\t| `mass` | Large lump | Scalpel ring, Forceps lift, leaves a `wound` | .2 | |\n365\t| `hook` | Barbed steel | Scalpel relief cuts at each barb, then Forceps | .3 | Tears if pulled with barbs set |\n366\t| `clot` | Dark lump in a vessel | Targeted Syringe, then Forceps | .3 | |\n367\t| `patch` | Inflamed red hatch | Salve | .1 | |\n368\t| `nerve`, `eloquent` | Gold line (no-touch) | Avoid | | Touching with Scalpel or Cautery: -12 and a slip |\n369\t| `retractorHandle` | Steel handle (Grand Rounds) | Forceps hold | | Opens a window on a target while held |\n370\t| device parts | Screws, wires, leads (Case 9) | Per Case 9 | | |\n371\t\n372\tObjects can be **hidden** (need Sonar), **under a pool**, or **tied** to a phase script. Phase scripts are data: `at: [{ t: 25, do: 'lurch' }]`, `when: 'clear'`.\n373\t\n374\t---\n375\t\n376\t## 8. HUSH: the strains\n377\t\n378\t**HUSH** stands for *Hyperplastic Undifferentiated Synaptic Hive*: an engineered parasite colony that feeds on pain signals and leaves its host blissful, numb and dying. Strains are named after musical markings, because they sing. Chief Oyelaran: \"They sing, Doctor. So we name them like songs.\"\n379\t\n380\t**Common look and sound.** Teal bioluminescence (`#5ff0e0`) with a dark core (`#0b2a30`), additive halo, lavender on bosses. Teal always means HUSH. They stay bright in Long Breath's cold palette. Every strain has its own figure in the choir voice (18), on the **effects** bus: music defaults to 10%, so no gameplay information may depend on music.\n381\t\n382\t### Staccato (Case 4 on)\n383\tSwarm of beads hopping in bursts on a fixed beat, from hidden **nests**. A closing dotted ring shows each landing spot .5 s ahead. A landing bite leaves a `laceration`. Cautery kills a bead in .12 s. Find nests with Sonar; slash, then Forceps. When the last nest goes, the remaining beads die within 3 s. Sound: \"tk-tk-tk\" on the beat, so the rhythm is playable by ear.\n384\t\n385\t### Legato (Case 5 on)\n386\tA long worm under the tissue, seen as a moving ridge (the painter's `stampLine` with positive height). Its head shows on Sonar.\n387\t1. Three slur marks lie on its route. Cut the short guide as the ridge nears one; it surfaces.\n388\t2. Forceps grips the head. Pull slowly: a tension gauge shows the safe rate. Too fast snaps it: the rest regrows a head and flees.\n389\t3. Every 4th segment has a barb. Release in the hold zone so Quill clamps, burn the barb, regrip. Pull, clamp, burn, regrip is the rhythm of the fight.\n390\t4. At half length it splits once no matter what, and a smaller worm flees. Repeat, faster.\n391\t\n392\t### Tremolo (Case 6, first boss)\n393\tA shivering web around a coronary vessel on the beating heart, five **reeds** around a mass.\n394\t- **Aura:** within 70 u the cursor jitters by up to ±5 u x `amp`; x.35 in fine mode, none in Long Breath. Vitals shake ±3 and drain .6/s x `amp`.\n395\t- **Stage 1:** after each heartbeat's peak the reeds flare for .3 s. A targeted Syringe in that window sedates one (`amp` -.2). Outside the window it spits a small cut. The beat, the ECG and the flare sound are the telegraph, so this plays like a rhythm game.\n396\t- **Stage 2:** at `amp` .2 or less the shell cracks. Cautery burns the mass (3 s in all) while reeds wake every 6 s and need sedating again. Below half, it tears free and crawls along the vessel leaving cuts. Holding it with Forceps pins it for 2 s.\n397\t- **Stage 3:** a last slow Forceps pull of 40 u.\n398\t\n399\t### Da Capo (Case 8)\n400\tThree **lobes** on a glowing staff line with repeat signs. Each lobe's armor opens on its own timer: A every 6 s for 2.5 s, B every 7 s for 2.5 s, C every 9 s for 3 s. A: Cautery 1 s. B: slash, then Forceps. C: targeted Syringe, then Salve 1 s. The first kill starts a 10 s countdown. At 0 the dead lobes regrow, \"from the top\": vitals -8, a repeat sign flashes, and the staff plays its figure again. Round 2 runs at double speed and adds Staccato beads. Second Look's ghosts show when each lobe will open, so the winning move is to rewind once and plan the order. It can be beaten without rewinding, on skill.\n401\t\n402\t### Fermata (Case 10)\n403\tAn eye-like organism in the gyri of the brain, among gold eloquent lines.\n404\t- It raises **hold domes** of radius 34, up to three, for 5 s each. Inside a dome everything waits: tools do nothing, and bleeders inside are paused too, which a clever player can use.\n405\t- Its eye opens for .7 s the moment a dome collapses. Cautery .3 s three times. Long Breath stretches that window to 2.8 s, and Second Look undoes a slip across an eloquent line. Both matter, so this is where Consult is taught.\n406\t- Stage 2: the eye flees to another gyrus (find it with Sonar, where Vale's radius helps) and two Legato slivers appear.\n407\t- Stage 3: a gentle Forceps pull with a very low tension limit.\n408\t\n409\t### Cantor (Case 11, final)\n410\tA vast choir-organ of tissue wrapped around the heart, three mouths on the great vessels. Clock refills between movements.\n411\t\n412\t1. **Exposition.** It conducts: a baton tendril draws glowing arcs, and each beat a section sings: Staccato from one mouth, Legato slivers from another, bleeders along the vessels. Slash three baton tendrils at the pause of each arc. Then burn each mouth's uvula (Cautery 1.5 s).\n413\t2. **Development.** The hush falls. **All sound cuts except the choir, and the monitor goes to static:** no number, no trace, no beep, for stretches of 15 s. Only Quill's readouts remain (\"Forty, Doctor.\"), plus the tissue's pulse. Fermata domes and Tremolo jitter combine. Find the voice box with Sonar and sedate it on beat windows, three times. This is the game's most memorable moment. Make it feel terrible and wonderful.\n414\t3. **Recapitulation.** Every 20 s it forcibly rewinds the field 6 s: everything you did is undone, though your Steady stays. A 3 s wind-up shows a giant repeat sign. Slash it during the wind-up (Long Breath makes it easy), or answer with Second Look right after, undoing its rewind (\"I've seen this one.\"). Six slashes or counters end it.\n415\t4. **Coda: the Last Note.** One spiral guide, 200 u long, over the beating heart. Fine mode. The stroke can only advance between beats; moving on the beat is a slip. Then Forceps draws the Note out on a rising tone, up to a scream, then **total silence**. Hold it for three seconds. Then one beep. Then Quill: \"...Doctor.\" Six final stitches to a quiet piano figure, then the dressing.\n416\t\n417\t---\n418\t\n419\t## 9. The cases\n420\t\n421\tNumbers are Resident defaults, to be balanced with the bot and by hand. Each case's data: patient, doctor, site, clock, vitals, drain, par, phases, barks, story nodes. All text in sections 9 and 10 is direction: write the finished lines yourself in that voice.\n422\t\n423\t### 9.1 The Lightbox (Second Opinion)\n424\t\n425\tBefore Cases 2, 4 and 10. The chart carries someone's **first opinion**. On the lightbox is the film: procedural X-ray art (ribs, spine or skull in pale polygons, soft-tissue haze, noise, one anomaly). Odalys chats. Point and press to mark what's really wrong (within 14 u; three tries; no fail state).\n426\t\n427\t- Right: \"Second opinion confirmed.\" +20 s and +20 Steady, and a marker on the op's field.\n428\t- Wrong: -10 s, a Percy smirk, and try again.\n429\t\n430\tIt is short (20 to 40 s), it teaches pointing, and it is the source's subtitle turned into a mechanic.\n431\t\n432\t### 9.2 Chapter 1: Bedside Manner\n433\t\n434\t**Case 1, Glasshouse** (Dr. Vale, forearm; clock 180, vitals 80, drain .1). *New: cursor, wheel, trigger, four tools, grades.* Tutorial freezes on.\n435\t1. Pull the glass: 4 shards.\n436\t2. Clean the cuts: 3 lacerations, Salve.\n437\t3. Close: 1 wound, 4 stitches.\n438\t4. Give her a boost: a scripted dip to 40, Syringe to the IV. Dressing.\n439\t\n440\t**Case 2, Low Brass** (Vale, abdomen; 210, 70, .2). *New: Lightbox, Scalpel, Suction, Cautery, excision.* First opinion: Percy's \"Hernia. Obviously.\"\n441\t1. Open: 60 u guide.\n442\t2. Clear the field: 2 pools, 1 bleeder.\n443\t3. Burn the polyps: 5.\n444\t4. Take the mass: ring cut, lift. Underneath, a trombone spit-valve cork swallowed decades ago, which comes out with Forceps. Quill: \"I'll label it.\"\n445\t5. Close: 6 stitches, Salve.\n446\t\n447\t**Case 3, Crane Nine** (Vale, abdomen; 150, 55, .5). *New: triage, Code Blue, Long Breath.* The harbor crane has collapsed, and the Chief is away.\n448\t1. Stop the bleeding: 3 artery tears, bleeders, pools everywhere.\n449\t2. Shrapnel: 5 fragments, 2 under pools.\n450\t3. He's crashing: vitals ramp to 0, the guaranteed first Code Blue. On the revive the world goes quiet, rain hangs in the theatre window on the cutaway, and the prompt for Long Breath appears: six ruptures in a ring to seal within 12 s.\n451\t4. Close.\n452\t\n453\t### 9.3 Chapter 2: The Quiet Ones\n454\t\n455\t**Case 4, The Whistling Man** (Vale, flank; 200, 75, .25). *New: Sonar, barbed extraction, first strain.* Lightbox: the chart says superficial, and the film shows something moving.\n456\t1. Free the gaff: 2 relief cuts, a long axis pull.\n457\t2. Something moved: Sonar, 2 hidden nests.\n458\t3. Staccato.\n459\t4. Repair: 6 bites with Salve, Suture the gaff wound.\n460\t\n461\t**Case 5, Slur** (Vale, wrist with tendons and a gold nerve line; 220, 70, .2). *New: Legato, fine mode, no-touch anatomy, the clamp hand-off.*\n462\t1. Find it with Sonar.\n463\t2. Intercept at the slur marks.\n464\t3. The long pull with barbs.\n465\t4. It split.\n466\t5. Repair: Salve the track, 5 fine stitches.\n467\t\n468\t**Case 6, Bedside Manner** (Vale, heart; 240, 60, .35). *New: the beating heart, timing to the beat, cursor tremor, first boss.* The patient is the Chief.\n469\t1. Open: long sternal guide, Suction.\n470\t2. Tremolo.\n471\t3. Mend: a graft along the vessel, where stitches only land between beats. Salve.\n472\t\n473\t### 9.4 Chapter 3: Second Look\n474\t\n475\t**Case 7, Force Nine** (Dr. Sokolova, abdomen on the hospital ship; 170, 60, .35). *New: Second Look, the ship's roll, blackout.* `tilt` drifts pools at up to 25 u/s, and loose objects slide. The lamp sways: pass a moving `light` to the painter, so highlights roll around the field.\n476\t1. Winch damage: 4 fragments, 2 artery tears. At about 25 s a scripted lurch forces a slip. \"No. Again.\" Prompt: △. The ghost shows the lurch, and now you wait it out.\n477\t2. Blackout: the power fails, `dim` and a small `spot` follow the cursor. Find and seal 3 hidden bleeders by Sonar's lens alone.\n478\t3. Close on the roll: 8 stitches whose points shift ±6 u.\n479\t\n480\t**Case 8, From the Top** (Sokolova, abdomen; 210, 65, .3). *New: Da Capo, planning with ghosts.* The patient is Sister Perpetua.\n481\t1. Open, clear.\n482\t2. Da Capo, round 1.\n483\t3. Round 2.\n484\t4. Close.\n485\t\n486\t**Case 9, Dead Man's Rhythm** (Sokolova, chest wall with an implant; 200, 70, .25). *New: a device instead of a disease.* The patient is a whistleblower, whose pacemaker has been rigged to detonate if his heart stops or the can is forced. Vitals under 25 or clock at 0 sets it off: the ship goes, and you retry.\n487\t1. Expose the can: guide, Suction.\n488\t2. Four screws: Forceps turn, circling the stick or mouse three times per screw.\n489\t3. Read the board: the patient's halting instructions name a wire order, but Sonar shows the board differs. A real second opinion. Wires are marked ▲ ● ■, never by color alone.\n490\t4. Cautery solders a bypass lead, then Scalpel slashes the wires in the true order. A wrong wire: vitals -30, clock -30.\n491\t5. Lift it with Forceps at a very low tension limit while the ship rolls.\n492\t6. Close. Quill: \"I'll fetch the other forceps\" sets up the case, and \"We are doctors\" closes it.\n493\t\n494\t### 9.5 Chapter 4: Coda\n495\t\n496\t**Case 10, Eloquent Cortex** (Vale and Sokolova, brain; 240, 60, .3). *New: Consult.* Lightbox: Percy's chart on himself, in his own hand, says migraine. Fermata.\n497\t\n498\t**Case 11, Last Note** (both, mediastinum; 420, 70, .3). The Cantor. All tools, both gifts.\n499\t\n500\t### 9.6 Outside the story\n501\t\n502\t**Skills Lab** in Case Files: a sandbox with no clock and locked vitals, respawning one of everything unlocked so far. It reuses the op scene. Quill: \"This is a pig's trotter. It has signed the waiver.\"\n503\t\n504\t**Grand Rounds:** section 12.\n505\t\n506\t---\n507\t\n508\t## 10. Story bible\n509\t\n510\t### World\n511\t\n512\t**Port Anselm**, a rain-soaked North Atlantic harbor city, a few years from now. Gulls, foghorns, cranes. **St. Erasmus Harbor Hospital**, \"St. Raz's\" to the staff, is old, underfunded and proud, and its gleaming new east wing is paid for by the **Anodyne Foundation**, whose billboards say A LIFE WITHOUT PAIN. Its product is **Lull**, a miracle analgesic that really works. Its secret is HUSH.\n513\t\n514\t**Theme.** Pain is information. Pain means you're still here. A doctor's job is to listen to it, not abolish it. Motto among the staff who know: \"Don't let it go quiet.\"\n515\t\n516\t**The Steady.** An old theatre word, never said aloud in the lift. Surgeons who have it stretch a moment or take it back. Quill has passed instruments to three of them.\n517\t\n518\t### Cast\n519\t\n520\tPronouns as listed. Portraits per Appendix C.\n521\t\n522\t| Character | Role | Voice and manner | Look |\n523\t|---|---|---|---|\n524\t| **Dr. Imogen \"Immy\" Vale** (she), 26 | Second-year resident, first protagonist. Long Breath | Talks too fast, apologizes to unconscious patients, sure she's a fraud in clogs. Grows into quiet courage | Copper hair, freckles, teal cap and scrubs, lucky pen |\n525\t| **Bartholomew Quill** (he), 58 | Scrub nurse, Theatre 3, 31 years. The player's constant companion | Deadpan, unhurried, calls everyone \"Doctor\" in forty intonations. Knits. Ex-merchant navy | Grey moustache, blue scrubs, mask, heavy brows |\n526\t| **Dr. Marguerite Oyelaran** (she), 61 | Chief of Surgery | Dry, exacting, proud. Took the Foundation's money to keep the doors open, and takes Lull for her hands | Dark skin, white crop, gold glasses, white coat, cane |\n527\t| **Dr. Yevgenia \"Zhenya\" Sokolova** (she), 67 | Surgeon of the hospital ship *Samaritan's Wake*, second protagonist. Second Look | Gleeful, salty, ruthless with sentiment, pipe never lit. \"At my age, dear, everything happens twice.\" Unfinished business with Quill | White hair, face scar, olive fatigues, red headband |\n528\t| **Dr. Percival Whitlock** (he), 29 | Fellow resident, rival, author of wrong first opinions | Smug, handsome, terrified. Secretly on Lull to keep up | Perfect hair, pristine coat |\n529\t| **Odalys Ferreira** (she), 31 | Night radiology tech, runs the Lightbox | Excitable, conspiracy-minded, always right, always eating | Curls, oversized glasses, cardigan |\n530\t| **Dr. Lucian Hale** (he), 55 | Founder of the Anodyne Foundation. Beloved TV doctor | Soft, warm, perfect bedside voice. Never raises it. Means every word | Silver hair, tailored suit, kind eyes |\n531\t| **Clementine Hale** (she), 19 | His daughter, patient zero | Serene, smiling, has felt nothing since she was nine | Pale, cropped hair, hospital gown, faint teal under the skin |\n532\t\n533\t### How it sounds\n534\t\n535\tMelodrama played straight, as in the source: earnest speeches, jargon in bursts, cliffhanger reveals, sudden collapses, a villain who explains himself. Humor comes from character and never winks at the camera.\n536\t\n537\tRules:\n538\t- A line fits two rows of the dialog box, at most 90 characters. A scene runs 6 to 14 lines.\n539\t- Jargon in bursts, translated by someone immediately.\n540\t- Every patient gets one human detail.\n541\t- End scenes on a turn or a big sincere line.\n542\t- Tone choices now and then (Quill reacts; the story doesn't branch).\n543\t\n544\tSamples, for pitch. Use these and write hundreds more:\n545\t\n546\t- **Quill:** \"That was the liver, Doctor. We keep that one.\"\n547\t- **Quill:** \"Pulse forty. I mention it only in passing.\"\n548\t- **Quill:** \"I have seen worse. He was a plumber.\"\n549\t- **Quill:** \"Breathe, Doctor. One of you should.\"\n550\t- **Quill, after a Textbook stitch:** \"...Hm.\"\n551\t- **Vale:** \"Okay okay okay. Sorry! Sorry. She can't hear me. Sorry.\"\n552\t- **Vale:** \"My hands won't stop shaking.\" **Quill:** \"They stop around year thirty, Doctor. I'm told.\"\n553\t- **Oyelaran:** \"A surgeon who argues with a chart. How tiresome. Do it again.\"\n554\t- **Sokolova:** \"No. Again.\"\n555\t- **Sokolova:** \"In the navy we did this with a spoon.\"\n556\t- **Sokolova:** \"You're late, Bartholomew.\" **Quill:** \"Thirty-one years, Yevgenia.\"\n557\t- **Percy:** \"Hernia. Obviously.\"\n558\t- **Odalys:** \"Okay so don't freak out, but your X-ray is humming.\"\n559\t- **Hale:** \"Pain is the last disease, Doctor. I only ask you to stop defending it.\"\n560\t- **Ilse, waking:** \"It hurts. Oh, it hurts. Thank you.\"\n561\t\n562\t### Beats\n563\t\n564\t**Chapter 1: Bedside Manner.**\n565\t- *Case 1.* Night shift. Immy's first solo: **Agnes Pellow**, 74, fell through her greenhouse roof, and wants the glass kept for the grandchildren. Quill walks Immy through it. After: her hands shake.\n566\t- *Case 2.* **Desmond \"Slide\" Achebe**, trombonist at the Harbor Jazz Hall. Percy's chart says hernia. Odalys's film says otherwise. Immy argues. Inside: a mass, and a cork he swallowed in 1987. \"I wondered where that went.\" The Chief notices her.\n567\t- *Case 3.* Crane Nine falls. **Big Tam Rennie**, foreman, arrives on a gurney. Chief away, Immy alone. He dies on the table, then doesn't, and something happens to the time. After, Quill: \"How long did that feel, Doctor?\" \"A minute?\" \"Eleven seconds. We don't discuss it in the lift.\" The Chief, at last: \"There is an old word in theatre.\"\n568\t\n569\t**Chapter 2: The Quiet Ones.**\n570\t- *Case 4.* **Captain Ned Harrow** walks into the ER whistling with a gaff through his side, wants to get back to sea, feels nothing. Inside, something hops. Immy hears singing no one else hears. Quill, for the first time, says nothing. Odalys: \"It's not in any database.\" The Chief: not a word until she has spoken to the Foundation.\n571\t- *Case 5.* **Ilse Brandt**, 19, conservatory violinist, numb left hand, never happier. She doesn't want treatment. Legato. She wakes crying with pain, and thanks Immy. Odalys finds the pattern: every case went to an Anodyne clinic. Percy: \"You've been up for forty hours.\"\n572\t- *Case 6.* At the Foundation's gala in the new wing, with Hale speaking and chandeliers overhead, the Chief collapses, cane rolling across the floor. She has been hiding it. Immy operates on her mentor. Waking, the Chief confesses the bargain, names the disease, and gives Immy a name: Sokolova, on the *Samaritan's Wake*. Quill drops a tray for the first time in 31 years. On the ward TV, Hale: \"I hear Dr. Oyelaran is recovering. Wonderful.\" End of chapter.\n573\t\n574\t**Chapter 3: Second Look.** The player becomes Zhenya.\n575\t- *Case 7.* A North Atlantic storm. Immy and Quill arrive by launch as a winch crushes deckhand **Yusuf Demir**. Zhenya operates with a pipe in her teeth and shows Immy what the Steady can also do. Immy watches something un-happen.\n576\t- *Case 8.* **Sister Perpetua** runs the seamen's hospice, and brings aboard patients who've never been happier. Her strain regrows when cut. Afterward the hard argument, played fairly: \"You'd give them back their pain?\" Zhenya: \"I'd give them back their choice.\"\n577\t- *Case 9.* **Dr. Hollis Crane**, a Foundation researcher, comes aboard to confess, with a rigged pacemaker. Saved, he gives the last piece: it began with Hale's daughter. \"Everything is singing to her.\" They sail for Port Anselm.\n578\t\n579\t**Chapter 4: Coda.**\n580\t- *Case 10.* Percy collapses in Immy's arms: \"It's finally quiet.\" He has been on Lull all along. First time Immy and Zhenya operate together. Waking: \"Your stitches are crooked.\" Immy cries laughing.\n581\t- *Case 11.* Across the city, every Anodyne patient begins to fail at once. Hale carries his daughter into St. Raz's himself, his voice finally breaking: \"I only wanted her not to hurt.\" Everyone is there: the Chief in a wheelchair in the gallery, Percy on the monitors, Odalys at the film, Quill passing instruments. The last operation.\n582\t- *Ending.* Silence. A beep. Clementine wakes screaming, then laughing: she can feel her hands. The city wakes in pain, and St. Raz's ER is the happiest, loudest, most furious place on earth (\"Doctor, I have a headache!\" \"Wonderful!\"). Hale surrenders, gently. Immy looks at her hands. Quill: \"They've stopped shaking, Doctor.\" She looks again. \"No, they haven't.\" \"Good.\" Credits. After the credits, in Case Files, a new blank folder appears with a chart that reads *Incoming*, leaving the door open for more game without being a stub: it simply says \"Next shift starts at seven. Get some sleep, Doctor.\"\n583\t\n584\t**Barks.** Each case has lines for phase enter, idle hint, slip, low vitals, phase done. A shared pool of at least 40 Quill lines by situation, so play never repeats fast. Barks never pause play. Sokolova's scenes give barks to Quill or Immy as the scene suits.\n585\t\n586\t---\n587\t\n588\t## 11. The ward\n589\t\n590\tBetween cases the player walks **Ward 3** (Chapters 1, 2 and 4) or the **ship's ward** (Chapter 3, a smaller map with a swinging lamp). This second perspective makes the world real: the people you saved are in the beds.\n591\t\n592\t- `threequarter` view, `E.TileMap` from text rows, `E.Body`, `E.Humanoid` in scrubs at `size: 1.3`, E.Story for talk, lights on: fluorescent panels, a window wall with rain, a warm desk lamp. Start from `kits/adventure.js` for the movement and talk loop.\n593\t- **People:** saved patients appear in beds after their case, each with new lines per chapter (Agnes's grandchildren bring begonias; Slide practices with a mute). Odalys at her lightbox, Percy at the vending machine, Quill knitting, the Chief's door.\n594\t- **Input:** stick or WASD walks. ✕ or E talks when near. **Hover moves the focus to a person or door with an outline and a name, and clicking walks there** (`game.mouseGround()`, `E.FlowField`) and talks.\n595\t- **Theatre door:** a glowing NEXT CASE sign, at most 8 tiles from where you arrive, so the ward is never a chore. It's an optional breather.\n596\t- **Saves:** autosave on arrival, `game.saves.canSave = true` here, `menu: { saves: true }`.\n597\t- **Small reward:** talking to everyone before the next case starts it with +10 Steady (\"Morale,\" Quill says, as if it were a diagnosis).\n598\t\n599\t---\n600\t\n601\t## 12. Multiplayer: Grand Rounds\n602\t\n603\tOne case for 1 to 4 surgeons on one patient, each with their own cursor, wheel and glove color. The host runs the world. Team result, individual standings. About 4 minutes. It plays **the same on one couch and over the Hub.**\n604\t\n605\t### 12.1 The case\n606\t\n607\tA ferry disaster, mass casualty. Scales with N players:\n608\t1. **Triage:** 3N bleeders and tears, pools everywhere.\n609\t2. **Swarm:** Staccato, N nests, 4N beads at once.\n610\t3. **One holds, one cuts:** a `retractorHandle`: one player holds it with Forceps to open a window on a target another player cuts. Alone, Quill holds it at half the window, so solo still works.\n611\t4. **Choir:** a small three-lobed Da Capo.\n612\t5. **Close:** N wounds, then the dressing together.\n613\t\n614\tRules:\n615\t- Vitals and clock are shared. Steady is shared, and any player can call Long Breath for everyone. No Second Look in this mode.\n616\t- Each player's score sums their actions by grade, with slips subtracted. Standings by score. `won` is whether the patient lived.\n617\t- **Awards:** Steadiest Hand (Textbook rate), Needlework (stitches), Hoover (volume suctioned), Firebrand (burns).\n618\t- Seat colors follow the Hub's: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n619\t\n620\t### 12.2 On its own\n621\t\n622\tThe `lobby` scene: four seats. Player 1 is already in. `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })` lets a second pad join with Start or the keyboard's right half with 2. **Add AI colleague** fills a seat with a bot, so a solo player can see co-op and the attract mode can show it. The mouse belongs to player 1. One shared screen, no split. Each player's wheel appears around their own cursor.\n623\t\n624\t### 12.3 In a Hub session\n625\t\n626\tFollow the protocol page and Appendix D. Exact behavior:\n627\t- `session.js`, unmodified, is the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. Tags: `hub:session` 1, `hub:session-players` 1-4, `hub:session-mode` shared-world, `hub:session-minutes` 5, a goal of at most 60 characters, controls of at most 80.\n628\t- `AIHub.active`: no title, menus or attract. `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })` early, then `AIHub.ready()` with no key press when the world is built. `null` means no Hub: run as usual.\n629\t- **Your own card** (`card: true`): the theatre's whiteboard showing the game's name, each player in their color with a tick when ready, how to win, this player's controls from the device they used last, and the time left from the `card` event. Enter, ✕ or a tap answers `ready`. △ or M, named on the card, answers `more`. No menus, no settings, no choices. Use `AIHub.rng('card')` for anything random.\n630\t- `start`: count down on the card until `AIHub.time() >= 0`, then play.\n631\t- `Math.random` is not seeded in shared world, and the sim never uses it anyway: the host seeds from `AIHub.rng('sim')`.\n632\t- `leave`: drop that cursor, and rescale nothing mid-phase.\n633\t- `time-up`: the host calls `finish` at once, with `won: false` unless the patient is already closed. Patient closed or lost: the host sends a last reliable state, then `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won, stats, awards })`.\n634\t- `end`: no more input, no more sends, all sound off.\n635\t- `AIHub.server`: the host's copy has no player, camera or input. It runs the world and draws nothing.\n636\t- Rules: no chat, emotes or custom names, including pings. Names come from the Hub and are never inserted as HTML. No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open`, navigation, sockets or `fetch`. In a session the pause menu has only Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen (fact 15): no Quit, Restart or Dev Mode, and the world keeps running under it. The engine already ignores Options while Create is held.\n637\t\n638\t### 12.4 The network shape\n639\t\n640\tThe sim runs only on the host. Other copies keep a **mirror** with the same shape as the state, so the same draw code runs everywhere. Each copy moves its own cursor at once and never waits for the network.\n641\t\n642\t| Message | Direction | Rate and kind | Contents |\n643\t|---|---|---|---|\n644\t| `i` | Client → host | 15/s, unreliable | Sequence, cursor, tool, trigger, flags, up to 6 recent cursor samples so a stroke traces correctly at 150 ms latency |\n645\t| `p` | Client → host | Reliable, on press | Gift pressed |\n646\t| `s` | Host → all | 12/s, unreliable | Sequence, clock ms, vitals, phase, Steady, players, objects as short arrays. At most 1,100 bytes: rotate objects if needed, players always |\n647\t| `e` | Host → all | 4/s, reliable, batched | Presentation events (sound, popup, particles) and phase changes |\n648\t| `over` | Host → all | Once, reliable | Final state |\n649\t\n650\tThat stays within the limit of 20 messages a second per copy, bursts to 40. An unreliable message over about 1,200 bytes is lost whole if any packet drops. Drop old `s` by sequence number. Draw other players' cursors eased toward the last report.\n651\t\n652\t---\n653\t\n654\t## 13. Attract mode\n655\t\n656\tAfter 10 seconds with no input on `title`, `menu`, `casefiles` or `lobby`, start the demo; not on reading screens (About, Settings, Controls) or anywhere else. Track idleness with `game.hook('input', ...)`: any pressed action, held code, mouse move (`input.mouse.t`), click, or stick past .25.\n657\t\n658\t**A looping 2.5 minute reel, AI-played with cheats allowed** (locked vitals, instant wheel, perfect aim), showing as much as possible:\n659\t1. A story beat with portraits, auto-advanced."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3415d082-194f-4b08-a98a-256652a62b17","uuid":"46f7a84d-b948-4789-8e8d-327caaae200e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:03:00.083Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211908__x0e7d30/work/PLAN.md","content":"| Story: backlog | △ | B |\n\nSettings adds **Swap sticks** for left-handers (cursor and wheel trade sticks).\n\n```js\nconst { attack, dash, skill, jump, ...base } = E.Input.DEFAULT;\nconst INPUT = { ...base,\n  useTool: ['Pad7', 'Pad0', 'Mouse0', 'Space'], fine: ['Pad6', 'ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight'], gift: ['Pad3', 'KeyF', 'Mouse1'], consult: ['Pad2', 'KeyC'],\n  toolPrev: ['Pad4', 'KeyQ', 'WheelUp'], toolNext: ['Pad5', 'KeyE', 'WheelDown'], wheelHold: ['Mouse2'],\n  curU: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp'], curD: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown'], curL: ['KeyA', 'ArrowLeft'], curR: ['KeyD', 'ArrowRight'],   // separate from up/down/left/right, which the left stick also presses\n  nudgeU: ['Pad12'], nudgeD: ['Pad13'], nudgeL: ['Pad14'], nudgeR: ['Pad15'],\n  tool1: ['Digit1'], tool2: ['Digit2'], tool3: ['Digit3'], tool4: ['Digit4'], tool5: ['Digit5'], tool6: ['Digit6'], tool7: ['Digit7'], tool8: ['Digit8'],\n  interact: ['Pad0', 'KeyE', 'Enter'], skip: ['Pad2', 'ControlLeft'], log: ['Pad3', 'KeyB'], more: ['Pad3', 'KeyM'] };\n```\n\nRead the two sticks from `input.padAim` and `input.padMove`, the trigger from `input.value('useTool')`. Name the actions and hide `tool1` to `tool8` from rebinding through `menu.controls`. Give the scene `camera: { zoom: false, wheel: false }`, because the wheel is the game's.\n\n**Prompts.** Always through `game.input.label(action)`, so they follow the device used last and the player's rebinding. Draw a label as a button cap: a rounded rectangle with the label in it, the PlayStation or Xbox face colors on face buttons, and plain keycaps for keys. For the sticks, draw a small stick icon with L or R. Never hard-code a key in text. PlayStation glyphs by default with the Xbox option from fact 5.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Things on the table\n\nA registry (`defObject`), each with sim behavior, a draw function kept apart, and a bot plan.\n\n| Type | Looks like | Fixed by | Vitals/s while open | Other effects |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `shard` | Glass or steel sliver with a glint | Forceps along its axis | .1 | |\n| `laceration` | Short red cut | Salve | .2 | Small pool over time |\n| `wound` | Gaping cut with lips | Suture N stitches | .4 | Bleeding falls with each stitch |\n| `bleeder` | Pinhole spurting on the beat | Cautery | .5 | Feeds a pool, 6 volume/s |\n| `arteryTear` | Pulsing spurts | Suture 3 | 1 | Feeds a pool, 14 volume/s |\n| `pool` | Dark wet metaball, specular | Suction | none | Hides what's under it, drifts on a tilt |\n| `polyp` | Pink nub | Cautery | .1 | |\n| `mass` | Large lump | Scalpel ring, Forceps lift, leaves a `wound` | .2 | |\n| `hook` | Barbed steel | Scalpel relief cuts at each barb, then Forceps | .3 | Tears if pulled with barbs set |\n| `clot` | Dark lump in a vessel | Targeted Syringe, then Forceps | .3 | |\n| `patch` | Inflamed red hatch | Salve | .1 | |\n| `nerve`, `eloquent` | Gold line (no-touch) | Avoid | | Touching with Scalpel or Cautery: -12 and a slip |\n| `retractorHandle` | Steel handle (Grand Rounds) | Forceps hold | | Opens a window on a target while held |\n| device parts | Screws, wires, leads (Case 9) | Per Case 9 | | |\n\nObjects can be **hidden** (need Sonar), **under a pool**, or **tied** to a phase script. Phase scripts are data: `at: [{ t: 25, do: 'lurch' }]`, `when: 'clear'`.\n\n---\n\n## 8. HUSH: the strains\n\n**HUSH** stands for *Hyperplastic Undifferentiated Synaptic Hive*: an engineered parasite colony that feeds on pain signals and leaves its host blissful, numb and dying. Strains are named after musical markings, because they sing. Chief Oyelaran: \"They sing, Doctor. So we name them like songs.\"\n\n**Common look and sound.** Teal bioluminescence (`#5ff0e0`) with a dark core (`#0b2a30`), additive halo, lavender on bosses. Teal always means HUSH. They stay bright in Long Breath's cold palette. Every strain has its own figure in the choir voice (18), on the **effects** bus: music defaults to 10%, so no gameplay information may depend on music.\n\n### Staccato (Case 4 on)\nSwarm of beads hopping in bursts on a fixed beat, from hidden **nests**. A closing dotted ring shows each landing spot .5 s ahead. A landing bite leaves a `laceration`. Cautery kills a bead in .12 s. Find nests with Sonar; slash, then Forceps. When the last nest goes, the remaining beads die within 3 s. Sound: \"tk-tk-tk\" on the beat, so the rhythm is playable by ear.\n\n### Legato (Case 5 on)\nA long worm under the tissue, seen as a moving ridge (the painter's `stampLine` with positive height). Its head shows on Sonar.\n1. Three slur marks lie on its route. Cut the short guide as the ridge nears one; it surfaces.\n2. Forceps grips the head. Pull slowly: a tension gauge shows the safe rate. Too fast snaps it: the rest regrows a head and flees.\n3. Every 4th segment has a barb. Release in the hold zone so Quill clamps, burn the barb, regrip. Pull, clamp, burn, regrip is the rhythm of the fight.\n4. At half length it splits once no matter what, and a smaller worm flees. Repeat, faster.\n\n### Tremolo (Case 6, first boss)\nA shivering web around a coronary vessel on the beating heart, five **reeds** around a mass.\n- **Aura:** within 70 u the cursor jitters by up to ±5 u x `amp`; x.35 in fine mode, none in Long Breath. Vitals shake ±3 and drain .6/s x `amp`.\n- **Stage 1:** after each heartbeat's peak the reeds flare for .3 s. A targeted Syringe in that window sedates one (`amp` -.2). Outside the window it spits a small cut. The beat, the ECG and the flare sound are the telegraph, so this plays like a rhythm game.\n- **Stage 2:** at `amp` .2 or less the shell cracks. Cautery burns the mass (3 s in all) while reeds wake every 6 s and need sedating again. Below half, it tears free and crawls along the vessel leaving cuts. Holding it with Forceps pins it for 2 s.\n- **Stage 3:** a last slow Forceps pull of 40 u.\n\n### Da Capo (Case 8)\nThree **lobes** on a glowing staff line with repeat signs. Each lobe's armor opens on its own timer: A every 6 s for 2.5 s, B every 7 s for 2.5 s, C every 9 s for 3 s. A: Cautery 1 s. B: slash, then Forceps. C: targeted Syringe, then Salve 1 s. The first kill starts a 10 s countdown. At 0 the dead lobes regrow, \"from the top\": vitals -8, a repeat sign flashes, and the staff plays its figure again. Round 2 runs at double speed and adds Staccato beads. Second Look's ghosts show when each lobe will open, so the winning move is to rewind once and plan the order. It can be beaten without rewinding, on skill.\n\n### Fermata (Case 10)\nAn eye-like organism in the gyri of the brain, among gold eloquent lines.\n- It raises **hold domes** of radius 34, up to three, for 5 s each. Inside a dome everything waits: tools do nothing, and bleeders inside are paused too, which a clever player can use.\n- Its eye opens for .7 s the moment a dome collapses. Cautery .3 s three times. Long Breath stretches that window to 2.8 s, and Second Look undoes a slip across an eloquent line. Both matter, so this is where Consult is taught.\n- Stage 2: the eye flees to another gyrus (find it with Sonar, where Vale's radius helps) and two Legato slivers appear.\n- Stage 3: a gentle Forceps pull with a very low tension limit.\n\n### Cantor (Case 11, final)\nA vast choir-organ of tissue wrapped around the heart, three mouths on the great vessels. Clock refills between movements.\n\n1. **Exposition.** It conducts: a baton tendril draws glowing arcs, and each beat a section sings: Staccato from one mouth, Legato slivers from another, bleeders along the vessels. Slash three baton tendrils at the pause of each arc. Then burn each mouth's uvula (Cautery 1.5 s).\n2. **Development.** The hush falls. **All sound cuts except the choir, and the monitor goes to static:** no number, no trace, no beep, for stretches of 15 s. Only Quill's readouts remain (\"Forty, Doctor.\"), plus the tissue's pulse. Fermata domes and Tremolo jitter combine. Find the voice box with Sonar and sedate it on beat windows, three times. This is the game's most memorable moment. Make it feel terrible and wonderful.\n3. **Recapitulation.** Every 20 s it forcibly rewinds the field 6 s: everything you did is undone, though your Steady stays. A 3 s wind-up shows a giant repeat sign. Slash it during the wind-up (Long Breath makes it easy), or answer with Second Look right after, undoing its rewind (\"I've seen this one.\"). Six slashes or counters end it.\n4. **Coda: the Last Note.** One spiral guide, 200 u long, over the beating heart. Fine mode. The stroke can only advance between beats; moving on the beat is a slip. Then Forceps draws the Note out on a rising tone, up to a scream, then **total silence**. Hold it for three seconds. Then one beep. Then Quill: \"...Doctor.\" Six final stitches to a quiet piano figure, then the dressing.\n\n---\n\n## 9. The cases\n\nNumbers are Resident defaults, to be balanced with the bot and by hand. Each case's data: patient, doctor, site, clock, vitals, drain, par, phases, barks, story nodes. All text in sections 9 and 10 is direction: write the finished lines yourself in that voice.\n\n### 9.1 The Lightbox (Second Opinion)\n\nBefore Cases 2, 4 and 10. The chart carries someone's **first opinion**. On the lightbox is the film: procedural X-ray art (ribs, spine or skull in pale polygons, soft-tissue haze, noise, one anomaly). Odalys chats. Point and press to mark what's really wrong (within 14 u; three tries; no fail state).\n\n- Right: \"Second opinion confirmed.\" +20 s and +20 Steady, and a marker on the op's field.\n- Wrong: -10 s, a Percy smirk, and try again.\n\nIt is short (20 to 40 s), it teaches pointing, and it is the source's subtitle turned into a mechanic.\n\n### 9.2 Chapter 1: Bedside Manner\n\n**Case 1, Glasshouse** (Dr. Vale, forearm; clock 180, vitals 80, drain .1). *New: cursor, wheel, trigger, four tools, grades.* Tutorial freezes on.\n1. Pull the glass: 4 shards.\n2. Clean the cuts: 3 lacerations, Salve.\n3. Close: 1 wound, 4 stitches.\n4. Give her a boost: a scripted dip to 40, Syringe to the IV. Dressing.\n\n**Case 2, Low Brass** (Vale, abdomen; 210, 70, .2). *New: Lightbox, Scalpel, Suction, Cautery, excision.* First opinion: Percy's \"Hernia. Obviously.\"\n1. Open: 60 u guide.\n2. Clear the field: 2 pools, 1 bleeder.\n3. Burn the polyps: 5.\n4. Take the mass: ring cut, lift. Underneath, a trombone spit-valve cork swallowed decades ago, which comes out with Forceps. Quill: \"I'll label it.\"\n5. Close: 6 stitches, Salve.\n\n**Case 3, Crane Nine** (Vale, abdomen; 150, 55, .5). *New: triage, Code Blue, Long Breath.* The harbor crane has collapsed, and the Chief is away.\n1. Stop the bleeding: 3 artery tears, bleeders, pools everywhere.\n2. Shrapnel: 5 fragments, 2 under pools.\n3. He's crashing: vitals ramp to 0, the guaranteed first Code Blue. On the revive the world goes quiet, rain hangs in the theatre window on the cutaway, and the prompt for Long Breath appears: six ruptures in a ring to seal within 12 s.\n4. Close.\n\n### 9.3 Chapter 2: The Quiet Ones\n\n**Case 4, The Whistling Man** (Vale, flank; 200, 75, .25). *New: Sonar, barbed extraction, first strain.* Lightbox: the chart says superficial, and the film shows something moving.\n1. Free the gaff: 2 relief cuts, a long axis pull.\n2. Something moved: Sonar, 2 hidden nests.\n3. Staccato.\n4. Repair: 6 bites with Salve, Suture the gaff wound.\n\n**Case 5, Slur** (Vale, wrist with tendons and a gold nerve line; 220, 70, .2). *New: Legato, fine mode, no-touch anatomy, the clamp hand-off.*\n1. Find it with Sonar.\n2. Intercept at the slur marks.\n3. The long pull with barbs.\n4. It split.\n5. Repair: Salve the track, 5 fine stitches.\n\n**Case 6, Bedside Manner** (Vale, heart; 240, 60, .35). *New: the beating heart, timing to the beat, cursor tremor, first boss.* The patient is the Chief.\n1. Open: long sternal guide, Suction.\n2. Tremolo.\n3. Mend: a graft along the vessel, where stitches only land between beats. Salve.\n\n### 9.4 Chapter 3: Second Look\n\n**Case 7, Force Nine** (Dr. Sokolova, abdomen on the hospital ship; 170, 60, .35). *New: Second Look, the ship's roll, blackout.* `tilt` drifts pools at up to 25 u/s, and loose objects slide. The lamp sways: pass a moving `light` to the painter, so highlights roll around the field.\n1. Winch damage: 4 fragments, 2 artery tears. At about 25 s a scripted lurch forces a slip. \"No. Again.\" Prompt: △. The ghost shows the lurch, and now you wait it out.\n2. Blackout: the power fails, `dim` and a small `spot` follow the cursor. Find and seal 3 hidden bleeders by Sonar's lens alone.\n3. Close on the roll: 8 stitches whose points shift ±6 u.\n\n**Case 8, From the Top** (Sokolova, abdomen; 210, 65, .3). *New: Da Capo, planning with ghosts.* The patient is Sister Perpetua.\n1. Open, clear.\n2. Da Capo, round 1.\n3. Round 2.\n4. Close.\n\n**Case 9, Dead Man's Rhythm** (Sokolova, chest wall with an implant; 200, 70, .25). *New: a device instead of a disease.* The patient is a whistleblower, whose pacemaker has been rigged to detonate if his heart stops or the can is forced. Vitals under 25 or clock at 0 sets it off: the ship goes, and you retry.\n1. Expose the can: guide, Suction.\n2. Four screws: Forceps turn, circling the stick or mouse three times per screw.\n3. Read the board: the patient's halting instructions name a wire order, but Sonar shows the board differs. A real second opinion. Wires are marked ▲ ● ■, never by color alone.\n4. Cautery solders a bypass lead, then Scalpel slashes the wires in the true order. A wrong wire: vitals -30, clock -30.\n5. Lift it with Forceps at a very low tension limit while the ship rolls.\n6. Close. Quill: \"I'll fetch the other forceps\" sets up the case, and \"We are doctors\" closes it.\n\n### 9.5 Chapter 4: Coda\n\n**Case 10, Eloquent Cortex** (Vale and Sokolova, brain; 240, 60, .3). *New: Consult.* Lightbox: Percy's chart on himself, in his own hand, says migraine. Fermata.\n\n**Case 11, Last Note** (both, mediastinum; 420, 70, .3). The Cantor. All tools, both gifts.\n\n### 9.6 Outside the story\n\n**Skills Lab** in Case Files: a sandbox with no clock and locked vitals, respawning one of everything unlocked so far. It reuses the op scene. Quill: \"This is a pig's trotter. It has signed the waiver.\"\n\n**Grand Rounds:** section 12.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Story bible\n\n### World\n\n**Port Anselm**, a rain-soaked North Atlantic harbor city, a few years from now. Gulls, foghorns, cranes. **St. Erasmus Harbor Hospital**, \"St. Raz's\" to the staff, is old, underfunded and proud, and its gleaming new east wing is paid for by the **Anodyne Foundation**, whose billboards say A LIFE WITHOUT PAIN. Its product is **Lull**, a miracle analgesic that really works. Its secret is HUSH.\n\n**Theme.** Pain is information. Pain means you're still here. A doctor's job is to listen to it, not abolish it. Motto among the staff who know: \"Don't let it go quiet.\"\n\n**The Steady.** An old theatre word, never said aloud in the lift. Surgeons who have it stretch a moment or take it back. Quill has passed instruments to three of them.\n\n### Cast\n\nPronouns as listed. Portraits per Appendix C.\n\n| Character | Role | Voice and manner | Look |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Dr. Imogen \"Immy\" Vale** (she), 26 | Second-year resident, first protagonist. Long Breath | Talks too fast, apologizes to unconscious patients, sure she's a fraud in clogs. Grows into quiet courage | Copper hair, freckles, teal cap and scrubs, lucky pen |\n| **Bartholomew Quill** (he), 58 | Scrub nurse, Theatre 3, 31 years. The player's constant companion | Deadpan, unhurried, calls everyone \"Doctor\" in forty intonations. Knits. Ex-merchant navy | Grey moustache, blue scrubs, mask, heavy brows |\n| **Dr. Marguerite Oyelaran** (she), 61 | Chief of Surgery | Dry, exacting, proud. Took the Foundation's money to keep the doors open, and takes Lull for her hands | Dark skin, white crop, gold glasses, white coat, cane |\n| **Dr. Yevgenia \"Zhenya\" Sokolova** (she), 67 | Surgeon of the hospital ship *Samaritan's Wake*, second protagonist. Second Look | Gleeful, salty, ruthless with sentiment, pipe never lit. \"At my age, dear, everything happens twice.\" Unfinished business with Quill | White hair, face scar, olive fatigues, red headband |\n| **Dr. Percival Whitlock** (he), 29 | Fellow resident, rival, author of wrong first opinions | Smug, handsome, terrified. Secretly on Lull to keep up | Perfect hair, pristine coat |\n| **Odalys Ferreira** (she), 31 | Night radiology tech, runs the Lightbox | Excitable, conspiracy-minded, always right, always eating | Curls, oversized glasses, cardigan |\n| **Dr. Lucian Hale** (he), 55 | Founder of the Anodyne Foundation. Beloved TV doctor | Soft, warm, perfect bedside voice. Never raises it. Means every word | Silver hair, tailored suit, kind eyes |\n| **Clementine Hale** (she), 19 | His daughter, patient zero | Serene, smiling, has felt nothing since she was nine | Pale, cropped hair, hospital gown, faint teal under the skin |\n\n### How it sounds\n\nMelodrama played straight, as in the source: earnest speeches, jargon in bursts, cliffhanger reveals, sudden collapses, a villain who explains himself. Humor comes from character and never winks at the camera.\n\nRules:\n- A line fits two rows of the dialog box, at most 90 characters. A scene runs 6 to 14 lines.\n- Jargon in bursts, translated by someone immediately.\n- Every patient gets one human detail.\n- End scenes on a turn or a big sincere line.\n- Tone choices now and then (Quill reacts; the story doesn't branch).\n\nSamples, for pitch. Use these and write hundreds more:\n\n- **Quill:** \"That was the liver, Doctor. We keep that one.\"\n- **Quill:** \"Pulse forty. I mention it only in passing.\"\n- **Quill:** \"I have seen worse. He was a plumber.\"\n- **Quill:** \"Breathe, Doctor. One of you should.\"\n- **Quill, after a Textbook stitch:** \"...Hm.\"\n- **Vale:** \"Okay okay okay. Sorry! Sorry. She can't hear me. Sorry.\"\n- **Vale:** \"My hands won't stop shaking.\" **Quill:** \"They stop around year thirty, Doctor. I'm told.\"\n- **Oyelaran:** \"A surgeon who argues with a chart. How tiresome. Do it again.\"\n- **Sokolova:** \"No. Again.\"\n- **Sokolova:** \"In the navy we did this with a spoon.\"\n- **Sokolova:** \"You're late, Bartholomew.\" **Quill:** \"Thirty-one years, Yevgenia.\"\n- **Percy:** \"Hernia. Obviously.\"\n- **Odalys:** \"Okay so don't freak out, but your X-ray is humming.\"\n- **Hale:** \"Pain is the last disease, Doctor. I only ask you to stop defending it.\"\n- **Ilse, waking:** \"It hurts. Oh, it hurts. Thank you.\"\n\n### Beats\n\n**Chapter 1: Bedside Manner.**\n- *Case 1.* Night shift. Immy's first solo: **Agnes Pellow**, 74, fell through her greenhouse roof, and wants the glass kept for the grandchildren. Quill walks Immy through it. After: her hands shake.\n- *Case 2.* **Desmond \"Slide\" Achebe**, trombonist at the Harbor Jazz Hall. Percy's chart says hernia. Odalys's film says otherwise. Immy argues. Inside: a mass, and a cork he swallowed in 1987. \"I wondered where that went.\" The Chief notices her.\n- *Case 3.* Crane Nine falls. **Big Tam Rennie**, foreman, arrives on a gurney. Chief away, Immy alone. He dies on the table, then doesn't, and something happens to the time. After, Quill: \"How long did that feel, Doctor?\" \"A minute?\" \"Eleven seconds. We don't discuss it in the lift.\" The Chief, at last: \"There is an old word in theatre.\"\n\n**Chapter 2: The Quiet Ones.**\n- *Case 4.* **Captain Ned Harrow** walks into the ER whistling with a gaff through his side, wants to get back to sea, feels nothing. Inside, something hops. Immy hears singing no one else hears. Quill, for the first time, says nothing. Odalys: \"It's not in any database.\" The Chief: not a word until she has spoken to the Foundation.\n- *Case 5.* **Ilse Brandt**, 19, conservatory violinist, numb left hand, never happier. She doesn't want treatment. Legato. She wakes crying with pain, and thanks Immy. Odalys finds the pattern: every case went to an Anodyne clinic. Percy: \"You've been up for forty hours.\"\n- *Case 6.* At the Foundation's gala in the new wing, with Hale speaking and chandeliers overhead, the Chief collapses, cane rolling across the floor. She has been hiding it. Immy operates on her mentor. Waking, the Chief confesses the bargain, names the disease, and gives Immy a name: Sokolova, on the *Samaritan's Wake*. Quill drops a tray for the first time in 31 years. On the ward TV, Hale: \"I hear Dr. Oyelaran is recovering. Wonderful.\" End of chapter.\n\n**Chapter 3: Second Look.** The player becomes Zhenya.\n- *Case 7.* A North Atlantic storm. Immy and Quill arrive by launch as a winch crushes deckhand **Yusuf Demir**. Zhenya operates with a pipe in her teeth and shows Immy what the Steady can also do. Immy watches something un-happen.\n- *Case 8.* **Sister Perpetua** runs the seamen's hospice, and brings aboard patients who've never been happier. Her strain regrows when cut. Afterward the hard argument, played fairly: \"You'd give them back their pain?\" Zhenya: \"I'd give them back their choice.\"\n- *Case 9.* **Dr. Hollis Crane**, a Foundation researcher, comes aboard to confess, with a rigged pacemaker. Saved, he gives the last piece: it began with Hale's daughter. \"Everything is singing to her.\" They sail for Port Anselm.\n\n**Chapter 4: Coda.**\n- *Case 10.* Percy collapses in Immy's arms: \"It's finally quiet.\" He has been on Lull all along. First time Immy and Zhenya operate together. Waking: \"Your stitches are crooked.\" Immy cries laughing.\n- *Case 11.* Across the city, every Anodyne patient begins to fail at once. Hale carries his daughter into St. Raz's himself, his voice finally breaking: \"I only wanted her not to hurt.\" Everyone is there: the Chief in a wheelchair in the gallery, Percy on the monitors, Odalys at the film, Quill passing instruments. The last operation.\n- *Ending.* Silence. A beep. Clementine wakes screaming, then laughing: she can feel her hands. The city wakes in pain, and St. Raz's ER is the happiest, loudest, most furious place on earth (\"Doctor, I have a headache!\" \"Wonderful!\"). Hale surrenders, gently. Immy looks at her hands. Quill: \"They've stopped shaking, Doctor.\" She looks again. \"No, they haven't.\" \"Good.\" Credits. After the credits, in Case Files, a new blank folder appears with a chart that reads *Incoming*, leaving the door open for more game without being a stub: it simply says \"Next shift starts at seven. Get some sleep, Doctor.\"\n\n**Barks.** Each case has lines for phase enter, idle hint, slip, low vitals, phase done. A shared pool of at least 40 Quill lines by situation, so play never repeats fast. Barks never pause play. Sokolova's scenes give barks to Quill or Immy as the scene suits.\n\n---\n\n## 11. The ward\n\nBetween cases the player walks **Ward 3** (Chapters 1, 2 and 4) or the **ship's ward** (Chapter 3, a smaller map with a swinging lamp). This second perspective makes the world real: the people you saved are in the beds.\n\n- `threequarter` view, `E.TileMap` from text rows, `E.Body`, `E.Humanoid` in scrubs at `size: 1.3`, E.Story for talk, lights on: fluorescent panels, a window wall with rain, a warm desk lamp. Start from `kits/adventure.js` for the movement and talk loop.\n- **People:** saved patients appear in beds after their case, each with new lines per chapter (Agnes's grandchildren bring begonias; Slide practices with a mute). Odalys at her lightbox, Percy at the vending machine, Quill knitting, the Chief's door.\n- **Input:** stick or WASD walks. ✕ or E talks when near. **Hover moves the focus to a person or door with an outline and a name, and clicking walks there** (`game.mouseGround()`, `E.FlowField`) and talks.\n- **Theatre door:** a glowing NEXT CASE sign, at most 8 tiles from where you arrive, so the ward is never a chore. It's an optional breather.\n- **Saves:** autosave on arrival, `game.saves.canSave = true` here, `menu: { saves: true }`.\n- **Small reward:** talking to everyone before the next case starts it with +10 Steady (\"Morale,\" Quill says, as if it were a diagnosis).\n\n---\n\n## 12. Multiplayer: Grand Rounds\n\nOne case for 1 to 4 surgeons on one patient, each with their own cursor, wheel and glove color. The host runs the world. Team result, individual standings. About 4 minutes. It plays **the same on one couch and over the Hub.**\n\n### 12.1 The case\n\nA ferry disaster, mass casualty. Scales with N players:\n1. **Triage:** 3N bleeders and tears, pools everywhere.\n2. **Swarm:** Staccato, N nests, 4N beads at once.\n3. **One holds, one cuts:** a `retractorHandle`: one player holds it with Forceps to open a window on a target another player cuts. Alone, Quill holds it at half the window, so solo still works.\n4. **Choir:** a small three-lobed Da Capo.\n5. **Close:** N wounds, then the dressing together.\n\nRules:\n- Vitals and clock are shared. Steady is shared, and any player can call Long Breath for everyone. No Second Look in this mode.\n- Each player's score sums their actions by grade, with slips subtracted. Standings by score. `won` is whether the patient lived.\n- **Awards:** Steadiest Hand (Textbook rate), Needlework (stitches), Hoover (volume suctioned), Firebrand (burns).\n- Seat colors follow the Hub's: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### 12.2 On its own\n\nThe `lobby` scene: four seats. Player 1 is already in. `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })` lets a second pad join with Start or the keyboard's right half with 2. **Add AI colleague** fills a seat with a bot, so a solo player can see co-op and the attract mode can show it. The mouse belongs to player 1. One shared screen, no split. Each player's wheel appears around their own cursor.\n\n### 12.3 In a Hub session\n\nFollow the protocol page and Appendix D. Exact behavior:\n- `session.js`, unmodified, is the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. Tags: `hub:session` 1, `hub:session-players` 1-4, `hub:session-mode` shared-world, `hub:session-minutes` 5, a goal of at most 60 characters, controls of at most 80.\n- `AIHub.active`: no title, menus or attract. `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })` early, then `AIHub.ready()` with no key press when the world is built. `null` means no Hub: run as usual.\n- **Your own card** (`card: true`): the theatre's whiteboard showing the game's name, each player in their color with a tick when ready, how to win, this player's controls from the device they used last, and the time left from the `card` event. Enter, ✕ or a tap answers `ready`. △ or M, named on the card, answers `more`. No menus, no settings, no choices. Use `AIHub.rng('card')` for anything random.\n- `start`: count down on the card until `AIHub.time() >= 0`, then play.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded in shared world, and the sim never uses it anyway: the host seeds from `AIHub.rng('sim')`.\n- `leave`: drop that cursor, and rescale nothing mid-phase.\n- `time-up`: the host calls `finish` at once, with `won: false` unless the patient is already closed. Patient closed or lost: the host sends a last reliable state, then `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won, stats, awards })`.\n- `end`: no more input, no more sends, all sound off.\n- `AIHub.server`: the host's copy has no player, camera or input. It runs the world and draws nothing.\n- Rules: no chat, emotes or custom names, including pings. Names come from the Hub and are never inserted as HTML. No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open`, navigation, sockets or `fetch`. In a session the pause menu has only Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen (fact 15): no Quit, Restart or Dev Mode, and the world keeps running under it. The engine already ignores Options while Create is held.\n\n### 12.4 The network shape\n\nThe sim runs only on the host. Other copies keep a **mirror** with the same shape as the state, so the same draw code runs everywhere. Each copy moves its own cursor at once and never waits for the network.\n\n| Message | Direction | Rate and kind | Contents |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `i` | Client → host | 15/s, unreliable | Sequence, cursor, tool, trigger, flags, up to 6 recent cursor samples so a stroke traces correctly at 150 ms latency |\n| `p` | Client → host | Reliable, on press | Gift pressed |\n| `s` | Host → all | 12/s, unreliable | Sequence, clock ms, vitals, phase, Steady, players, objects as short arrays. At most 1,100 bytes: rotate objects if needed, players always |\n| `e` | Host → all | 4/s, reliable, batched | Presentation events (sound, popup, particles) and phase changes |\n| `over` | Host → all | Once, reliable | Final state |\n\nThat stays within the limit of 20 messages a second per copy, bursts to 40. An unreliable message over about 1,200 bytes is lost whole if any packet drops. Drop old `s` by sequence number. Draw other players' cursors eased toward the last report.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Attract mode\n\nAfter 10 seconds with no input on `title`, `menu`, `casefiles` or `lobby`, start the demo; not on reading screens (About, Settings, Controls) or anywhere else. Track idleness with `game.hook('input', ...)`: any pressed action, held code, mouse move (`input.mouse.t`), click, or stick past .25.\n\n**A looping 2.5 minute reel, AI-played with cheats allowed** (locked vitals, instant wheel, perfect aim), showing as much as possible:\n1. A story beat with portraits, auto-advanced.","numLines":330,"startLine":330,"totalLines":1263}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PPXRqQFr2moXkutHP66MKt","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
