, character and level, but the prompt doesn't draw that line. (4) 'The title/menu screen' for attract mode: I had to decide that reading screens such as About and Settings don't count, because a reel starting mid-sentence would be annoying.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Designing how a pointer-and-motion game feels on a stick at 10 feet with nobody here to feel it. The source lives on its pointer, so cursor speed, magnet, guide-following and the tool wheel are the game, and every number I gave for them is judgment rather than measurement. I could test that the input path works, but not that it feels good, so the plan tells builders to tune it first. Second hardest was scope: an hour of content where every case adds a new idea, ordered so builders of different strengths still end with a complete game, which is why the plan has milestones and an explicit cut order.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Say exactly what 'on its own' requires for multiplayer. Give a rough target length for the demo in minutes, since 'long enough to show all features' can mean ten minutes or two hours and that drives every scope decision. Name which screens count for attract mode. Split TASK.md into a builder part and a planner part. Pin the engine to a commit or version for the length of a batch: v0.89.0 shipped while I was planning and I only noticed when re-running my setup commands. It changed the menus, so I had to re-verify everything, and builders starting at different times may get different engines. Finally, say whether a plan may include code and how long it may be, because I had to guess that tested reference code would be welcome, and the plan ended up long.\"\n  }\n]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MC885BgQrwJqEmUBwPoung","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_typ