{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjCpBrD6s6psokF2xhLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The hook has a fair point: my coverage check only searched for keywords, which isn't a line-by-line check. Two of its objections don't apply, though. `STAGE.md` asks for a plan, not a build. It also sets the shape of `planner-exit-interview.json`, a different file from the builder's exit interview.\n\nI'll do the real audit now. Re-reading `TASK.md` against the plan already turns up small gaps:\n- It never says the engine may be changed.\n- The `pkill` ban isn't explicit.\n- The feedback questions and exit-interview fields aren't spelled out.\n\nI'll fix those first, then check every line."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1635,"cache_read_input_tokens":550444,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1635},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3415d082-194f-4b08-a98a-256652a62b17","uuid":"4fdcc53a-d67e-458e-a0ef-6506a7bd01f0","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:10:43.884Z","request_id":"req_011CgjCpBXcLX9aL7QonDbtQ"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjCpBrD6s6psokF2xhLr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NqYBo6ERTgZauUD7mS1anY","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 -I - <<'EOF'\np = 'PLAN.md'; s = open(p).read()\ndef rep(a, b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a) == 1, (s.count(a), a[:80])\n    s = s.replace(a, b)\n# Tech stack: the engine may be changed, and systems and assets added\nrep(\"\"\"no WebGPU, no 3D add-on, no CDN: the page must work from `file://` and inside the Hub with no network |\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"no WebGPU, no 3D add-on, no CDN: the page must work from `file://` and inside the Hub with no network. You may change anything in the handoff package, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs |\"\"\")\n# Performance wording\nrep(\"\"\"| **Performance** | Unlimited, as TASK.md says: no FPS targets, no optimization passes. Spend it on detail.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"| **Performance** | Unlimited, as TASK.md says: no FPS targets, no optimization passes. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as each scene needs.\"\"\")\n# Feature-complete, stated as the goal\nrep(\"\"\"**Core loop.** Read the field, call the right tool,\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"**What you are making.** A feature-complete demo: a complete, polished vertical slice, long enough to show every feature and mechanic, so a player clearly understands how the game plays and feels and what they can and cannot do. The rest of the game could then be built out with normal designer tools (19.3).\n\n**Core loop.** Read the field, call the right tool,\"\"\")\n# Keys PC players expect\nrep(\"\"\"The keyboard and mouse are native here, not stand-ins: the mouse is the best pointer in the game, and the pad is the best-feeling couch device.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"The keyboard and mouse are native here, not stand-ins for controller buttons: the same game on the same screens, with the keys PC players expect for this kind of game. The mouse is the best pointer in the game, and the pad is the best-feeling couch device.\"\"\")\n# Controller wording\nrep(\"\"\"Designed for a DualSense and a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse use the same screens.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Designed for a PS5 DualSense, or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping, on a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse use the same screens, not a separate desktop layout.\"\"\")\n# Exit interview, feedback questions, catalogue\nrep(\"\"\"Suggested catalogue values: type Game, genre Simulation, players 1–4, series Trauma Center, tags `2d`, `pixel art`, `story`, `co-op`, `boss fights`, `comedy`.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Field notes for Source Information: Date, Environment and Model come from your TASK.md as written. Source is the work this is based on. Plan is the file name of this plan. Tech lists the stack, libraries and engine with versions. Testing is the testing parameters TASK.md allowed. Research is what you actually did, or none. Usage is literally `added on import`: you can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it.\n\n**The exit interview's contents**, in TASK.md's exact shape, beside the HTML file: `sourceInformation` (the same fields), `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback` and `promptFeedback` (lists, one answer per question, in order), and `aiHub` (name as on the title screen, tagline, description, type, genre, source, series, players, controls, tags). Plain text, every value filled in, \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm, and check that it parses as JSON. The catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record, so they aren't asked for.\n\n**The questions, so `notes/feedback.md` can collect answers as you go** (TASK.md has the exact wording):\n- Testing: how many issues testing caught; how many were critical or game breaking; the most helpful test; the least helpful test.\n- Engine: what you liked most; whether you got stuck or guessed wrong; the most important thing you had to build yourself; the hardest part of using the engine; what you changed in it and why (\"none\" if nothing); the one thing you would change.\n- Prompt: what you liked most; what was confusing; the hardest thing to do; what you would change.\n\nSuggested catalogue values: type Game, genre Simulation, source Trauma Center: Second Opinion, series Trauma Center, players 1–4, controls as one line like the Hub's how-to, and 3 to 6 lowercase tags such as `2d`, `pixel art`, `story`, `co-op`, `boss fights`, `comedy`.\"\"\")\n# Engine's headless tool\nrep(\"\"\"- The built HTML plays 30 s with no errors in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"- The built HTML plays 30 s with no errors in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit.\n- `node tools/check.mjs` on the page (add `#scene` to jump to a scene): errors, what's on screen, look notes. Act on every look note.\"\"\")\n# Second player stand-in\nrep(\"\"\"- With 2 players in the harness, whose game frames are same-origin and reachable from the test: feed a client's cursor through a stroke, and check the stroke lands on the host's state.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"- This computer has no second player, so use the harness's frames, or two browser pages or contexts, as the stand-in. With 2 players in the harness, whose game frames are same-origin and reachable from the test: feed a client's cursor through a stroke, and check the stroke lands on the host's state.\"\"\")\n# Server and process rules, in full\nrep(\"\"\"A server only for the harness, on `127.0.0.1` with an OS-picked port; confirm the page is your game before trusting a result; stop only your own process by PID.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Other agents share this machine's ports and processes: use a server only for the harness, bound to `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it), and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\"\"\")\nopen(p, 'w').write(s); print('gap fixes applied;', len(s.split()), 'words')\nEOF","description":"Fix the coverage gaps found by re-reading the brief against the plan"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1635,"cache_read_input_tokens":550444,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1635},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3415d082-194f-4b08-a98a-256652a62b17","uuid":"e412ce36-cf2a-4af2-8dfd-8b942ecbb98b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:11:00.117Z","request_id":"req_011CgjCpBXcLX9aL7QonDbtQ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01NqYBo6ERTgZauUD7mS1anY":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211908__x0e7d30/work && python3 -I - <<'EOF'\np = 'PLAN.md'; s = open(p).read()\ndef rep(a, b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a) == 1, (s.count(a), a[:80])\n    s = s.replace(a, b)\n# Tech stack: the engine may be changed, and systems and assets added\nrep(\"\"\"no WebGPU, no 3D add-on, no CDN: the page must work from `file://` and inside the Hub with no network |\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"no WebGPU, no 3D add-on, no CDN: the page must work from `file://` and inside the Hub with no network. You may change anything in the handoff package, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs |\"\"\")\n# Performance wording\nrep(\"\"\"| **Performance** | Unlimited, as TASK.md says: no FPS targets, no optimization passes. Spend it on detail.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"| **Performance** | Unlimited, as TASK.md says: no FPS targets, no optimization passes. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows as each scene needs.\"\"\")\n# Feature-complete, stated as the goal\nrep(\"\"\"**Core loop.** Read the field, call the right tool,\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"**What you are making.** A feature-complete demo: a complete, polished vertical slice, long enough to show every feature and mechanic, so a player clearly understands how the game plays and feels and what they can and cannot do. The rest of the game could then be built out with normal designer tools (19.3).\n\n**Core loop.** Read the field, call the right tool,\"\"\")\n# Keys PC players expect\nrep(\"\"\"The keyboard and mouse are native here, not stand-ins: the mouse is the best pointer in the game, and the pad is the best-feeling couch device.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"The keyboard and mouse are native here, not stand-ins for controller buttons: the same game on the same screens, with the keys PC players expect for this kind of game. The mouse is the best pointer in the game, and the pad is the best-feeling couch device.\"\"\")\n# Controller wording\nrep(\"\"\"Designed for a DualSense and a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse use the same screens.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Designed for a PS5 DualSense, or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping, on a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse use the same screens, not a separate desktop layout.\"\"\")\n# Exit interview, feedback questions, catalogue\nrep(\"\"\"Suggested catalogue values: type Game, genre Simulation, players 1–4, series Trauma Center, tags `2d`, `pixel art`, `story`, `co-op`, `boss fights`, `comedy`.\"\"\",\n    \"\"\"Field notes for Source Information: Date, Environment and Model come from your TASK.md as written. Source is the work this is based on. Plan is the file name of this plan. Tech lists the stack, libraries and engine with versions. Testing is the testing parameters TASK.md allowed. Research is what you actually did, or none. Usage is literally `added on import`: you can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it.\n\n**The exit interview's contents**, in TASK.md's exact sh